window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["ugc-net-2013-dec"] = {"id": "ugc-net-2013-dec", "title": "UGC NET December 2013, Psychology Paper II", "fullTitle": "UGC NET December 2013, Psychology Paper II (booklet D-04-13), with the official UGC key", "sub": "50 Qs · the real NET paper · official key after grievances · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "The visible spectrum wavelength ranges from", "options": {"a": "380 to 760 nanometres", "b": "200 to 700 nanometres", "c": "400 to 1000 nanometres", "d": "100 to 1000 nanometres"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the visible spectrum, the band of electromagnetic energy the human eye can detect, runs from about 380 to 760 nanometres. A nanometre is one billionth of a metre. Light is only a thin slice of the electromagnetic spectrum, which also includes gamma rays, X-rays, microwaves and radio waves. Within the visible band, wavelength is experienced as hue: the shortest visible waves (about 380 to 490 nanometres) look violet and blue, the middle ones green and yellow, and the longest (about 620 to 760 nanometres) red. Just below the short end lies ultraviolet, which bees can see but we cannot; just beyond the long end lies infrared, which we feel as heat rather than see. Isaac Newton, in prism experiments he began in 1666, showed that white light is a mixture of these colours by spreading sunlight into a band and then recombining it. Textbooks round the limits differently, giving 380 or 400 at the short end and 700 or 760 at the long end, but every version keeps both ends between ultraviolet and infrared. Riya remembers it as 'roughly 400 to 700, VIBGYOR from short to long'. Option a is right because 380 to 760 nanometres is the standard textbook range for human vision. Exam trap: each wrong option contains a real stretch of visible light but extends it into invisible regions, so check both ends of the range, not just one.", "topic": "The visible spectrum", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Shyam does not like to be in family gatherings but his mother has asked him to attend it. He decides to tell a lie that he has examination on that day however he finds it very distasteful. Shyam is experiencing _____.", "options": {"a": "Double avoidance conflict", "b": "Double approach conflict", "c": "Approach-avoidance conflict", "d": "Double approach-double avoidance conflict"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Shyam is in a double avoidance (avoidance-avoidance) conflict, because both of his choices are unpleasant to him. Kurt Lewin (1935), in A Dynamic Theory of Personality, described conflict as being caught between forces in a person's life space. Goals that attract have positive valence; goals that repel have negative valence. In an avoidance-avoidance conflict a person must pick one of two evils. Shyam dislikes family gatherings, so attending is one negative goal. Escaping with a lie about an examination is the other, and he finds lying distasteful. Neal Miller (1944) showed with rats that the urge to avoid a goal grows steeply the closer one gets to it, so a person in this conflict vacillates: as Shyam moves toward attending, attending feels worse and he swings toward the lie; as he moves toward lying, the lie feels worse and he swings back. Typical outcomes are dithering, delay and 'leaving the field', for instance falling ill on the day or refusing to decide. Option a is right because neither option attracts Shyam; he is trying to avoid both. Exam trap: the lie looks like an escape route, so students call this approach-avoidance, but the lie is itself a second thing Shyam wants to avoid, not an attractive goal with a drawback. For contrast: approach-approach means two attractive goals, approach-avoidance means one goal with good and bad sides, and double approach-avoidance means two options that each have good and bad sides.", "topic": "Lewin's types of conflict", "fig": "Kurt Lewin"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Computational approach involves a rigorous, mathematically oriented analysis of certain aspects of visual perception derived largely from computer simulation and artificial intelligence. Reason (R): Neurophysiological approach argues that sensory and perceptual phenomena are best explained by known neural and physiological mechanisms serving sensory structures.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: both statements are true descriptions of two different approaches to perception, but the second does not explain the first. Perception textbooks list several approaches side by side: psychophysical, Gestalt, constructivist (Hermann von Helmholtz, 1867; Richard Gregory, 1970), direct or ecological (James Gibson, 1979), neurophysiological and computational. Judge A on its own: it is true. The computational approach, set out by David Marr (1982) in his book Vision, treats seeing as information processing that can be analysed mathematically, often by writing computer programs that do the same job. Marr asked three questions about any visual task: what is computed and why (computational level), by what step-by-step procedure (algorithmic level), and in what hardware (implementational level). He traced vision from a primal sketch of edges, through a 2.5-D sketch of surfaces seen from the viewer's position, to a 3-D model of the object. Judge R on its own: it is also true. The neurophysiological approach explains perception through known neural mechanisms, such as the feature detector cells found by David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959, 1962) or lateral inhibition in the retina. Now ask whether R explains A. It does not: R describes a separate school, not the reason the computational approach is mathematical. Arjun, writing an essay on perception, would put them under two headings, not link them as cause and effect. Option b is right because both statements are correct but simply sit side by side. Exam trap: when A and R both describe approaches from the same chapter, students assume a link; ask 'does R tell me why A is true?' before choosing a.", "topic": "Approaches to visual perception", "fig": "David Marr"}, {"q": "Having seen or heard a stimulus once may facilitate our recognizing it on a later occasion, even if we are not aware that this is happening. This phenomenon is referred to as", "options": {"a": "Primacy effect", "b": "Recency effect", "c": "Practice effect", "d": "Priming effect"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the priming effect is the unconscious ease in recognising or processing a stimulus because you met it earlier. In a typical study, people read a list of words such as ELEPHANT and later fill in word fragments such as E_E_H_N_. They complete fragments of words they saw earlier more often, even when they cannot say which words were on the list. Endel Tulving, Daniel Schacter and Heather Stark (1982) showed that this fragment-completion priming is independent of recognition memory. Daniel Schacter (1987) called this kind of retention implicit memory: memory shown in performance, not in conscious recollection. Repetition priming involves the same item; semantic priming involves a related one, so seeing BREAD speeds recognition of BUTTER. Strong evidence that priming is separate from conscious memory comes from amnesia. Elizabeth Warrington and Lawrence Weiskrantz (1970) found that amnesic patients who could not recall a list still benefited normally from earlier exposure when given word fragments or first letters as cues. Example: Meera hears a new song playing in a shop without paying attention, and the next day it seems oddly familiar and easy to sing along to. Option d is right because the stem stresses two features, easier later recognition and no awareness, and together those define priming. Exam trap: primacy and recency also involve earlier exposure, but they describe where an item sat in a list that is being recalled consciously, not unconscious facilitation.", "topic": "Priming and implicit memory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Naturalistic observation is the highly preferred method in ethological research. Reason (R): Naturalistic observation is free from observer-bias.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: naturalistic observation is indeed the preferred method of ethology, but it is not free from observer bias. Ethology is the biological study of animal behaviour in the animal's natural setting. Its founders, Konrad Lorenz, Niko Tinbergen and Karl von Frisch, shared the 1973 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for work such as imprinting in geese, fixed action patterns in sticklebacks and the waggle dance of honeybees. Judge A on its own: it is true. Ethologists want to see behaviour as it evolved to work, in the animal's own habitat, so they watch without interfering. Judge R on its own: it is false. Naturalistic observation is especially open to observer bias, where the observer's expectations shape what is noticed, recorded and interpreted. It also suffers from reactivity (animals or people behave differently when they know they are watched) and from anthropomorphism, reading human motives into animals. Researchers reduce bias with ethograms (clear lists of behaviour categories), video recordings, observers who do not know the hypothesis, and checks of interobserver agreement using Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960). Because R is false, the question of whether it explains A does not arise. Example: Kabir, studying langur monkeys, expects the males to be aggressive and so codes rough play as fighting, until a second coder who does not know his hypothesis disagrees. Option c is right because A is a true statement and R is a false one. Exam trap: 'natural' sounds objective, but no observation is free of bias; bias is reduced by method, not removed by the setting.", "topic": "Naturalistic observation and bias", "fig": ""}, {"q": "There are various kinds of cells in human body. Which one of the following is not a neuron?", "options": {"a": "Sensory", "b": "Glia", "c": "Inter", "d": "Motor"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: glia are not neurons; they are the support cells of the nervous system. Rudolf Virchow coined the word neuroglia, 'nerve glue', in 1856, because he thought these cells simply held neurons together. Glial cells do not carry action potentials in the way neurons do, but they do far more than hold things in place. Astrocytes, star-shaped cells, supply nutrients to neurons, help form the blood-brain barrier and mop up excess neurotransmitter. Oligodendrocytes wrap myelin around axons in the central nervous system, and Schwann cells do the same in the peripheral nervous system. Microglia act as the brain's immune cleaners, removing debris and dead cells. Neurons themselves are classified by function into three types. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry information from receptors toward the brain and spinal cord. Motor (efferent) neurons carry commands from the central nervous system to muscles and glands. Interneurons (association neurons) lie entirely within the brain and spinal cord, link sensory to motor neurons and are by far the most numerous neurons. Example: when Meera pulls her hand away from a hot pan, a sensory neuron carries the signal to the spinal cord, an interneuron relays it, and a motor neuron makes the arm muscles contract, while glia around all three supply and insulate them. Option b is right because glia are a separate class of cell, while sensory, inter and motor are the three functional types of neuron. Exam trap: glia sit beside neurons everywhere and are roughly as numerous, so they are easily mistaken for a neuron type; remember 'glue', not 'messenger'.", "topic": "Glia versus neuron types", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): During action potential, there is change in polarization of ions across neuronal membrane. Reason (R): All the negative ions are transported outside the neuronal membrane.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: A is true, because an action potential is a brief reversal of polarisation across the neuron's membrane, but R is false, because negative ions are not all transported outside. At rest the inside of a neuron is about -70 millivolts relative to the outside. Judge A on its own. When the membrane is depolarised to threshold (about -55 millivolts), voltage-gated sodium channels open and positive sodium ions (Na+) rush in, driving the inside up to about +40 millivolts. The sodium channels then close, and voltage-gated potassium channels let positive potassium ions (K+) flow out, so the membrane repolarises and briefly dips below rest (hyperpolarisation). Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked this out in the giant axon of the squid and shared the 1963 Nobel Prize. So the change in polarisation is real, and it is driven by the movement of positive ions. Judge R on its own. The large negative protein anions inside the cell cannot cross the membrane and stay inside throughout. The sodium-potassium pump moves positive ions (three Na+ out for every two K+ in), not negative ones. Chloride, the main small negative ion, is already concentrated outside. So R is false, and the question of whether it explains A does not arise. Example: Arjun remembers it as 'sodium in, potassium out, proteins stay put'. Option c is right because A is correct and R misdescribes the ions involved. Exam trap: R uses the right vocabulary (ions, membrane, transport) but names the wrong charge, so it sounds plausible while being false.", "topic": "Ionic basis of the action potential", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Individual differences in intelligence are due to", "options": {"a": "only genetic factors", "b": "only environmental factors", "c": "genetic and economic factors", "d": "many factors including genetic and environmental"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: individual differences in intelligence arise from many factors, both genetic and environmental, acting together. Behaviour genetics estimates the genetic share with twin and adoption studies. Identical twins, who share all their genes, have more similar IQ scores than fraternal twins, who share about half, and adopted children come to resemble their biological parents more than their adoptive parents as they grow. Heritability, the proportion of differences within a population linked to genetic differences, rises with age: a combined analysis of about 11,000 twin pairs led by Claire Haworth and Robert Plomin (2010) found about 0.4 at age 9 rising to about 0.66 by age 17. But heritability is a population statistic, not a fixed limit for any one person. The environment matters too: prenatal health, nutrition, schooling, a stimulating home, family income and freedom from toxins such as lead. James Flynn (1984, 1987) showed that average IQ scores rose by about three points per decade across many nations, far too fast for genes to explain. Genes and environment also work together: a curious child seeks out books, and the books then sharpen her abilities. Example: identical twins Riya and Meera are adopted into different homes; their scores stay close, yet the twin given better schooling scores a few points higher. Option d is right because it is the only option that includes both genetic and environmental causes without narrowing them. Exam trap: option c sounds balanced, but economic status is only one environmental factor; 'many factors including genetic and environmental' is the full interactionist answer.", "topic": "Heredity and environment in intelligence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Put the parts of the Brain in sequence, going from higher to lower level of brain: I. Metencephalon, II. Mesencephalon, III. Diencephalon, IV. Telencephalon, V. Myelencephalon", "options": {"a": "I, II, III, IV, V", "b": "II, IV, I, III, V", "c": "IV, III, II, I, V", "d": "III, II, I, V, IV"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: from higher to lower, the order is telencephalon (IV), diencephalon (III), mesencephalon (II), metencephalon (I) and myelencephalon (V). These names come from embryology. Early in development the front end of the neural tube swells into three primary vesicles: the forebrain (prosencephalon), midbrain (mesencephalon) and hindbrain (rhombencephalon). The forebrain then splits into the telencephalon and diencephalon, and the hindbrain into the metencephalon and myelencephalon, giving five divisions stacked from the top of the brain down to the spinal cord. The telencephalon becomes the cerebral hemispheres: the cortex, basal ganglia and limbic structures. The diencephalon ('between brain') becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus. The mesencephalon stays as the midbrain, with the tectum (superior and inferior colliculi), the tegmentum and the substantia nigra. The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum. The myelencephalon ('marrow brain') becomes the medulla oblongata, which joins the spinal cord. 'Higher' here means further from the spinal cord and more involved in complex functions such as thought and language; 'lower' means closer to the spinal cord and more concerned with basic life functions such as breathing and heart rate. Example: Kabir notices that once tel- is placed on top, the other four names happen to fall in alphabetical order (di-, mes-, met-, myel-). Option c is right because it is the only sequence that runs from the cerebral hemispheres down to the medulla. Exam trap: mesencephalon and metencephalon differ by a single letter; mes- is the midbrain, and met- is the pons and cerebellum below it.", "topic": "Five divisions of the brain", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Arrange the following layers of Retina in order of light travelling from environment to back of the eyeball: I. Ganglion cells, II. Cone receptors, III. Horizontal cells, IV. Bipolar cells", "options": {"a": "I, II, III, IV", "b": "II, III, IV, I", "c": "IV, II, III, I", "d": "I, IV, III, II"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: light entering the eye passes through the ganglion cells (I) first, then the bipolar cells (IV), then the horizontal cells (III), and only then reaches the cone receptors (II). The vertebrate retina is built 'inside out'. The photoreceptors, rods and cones, lie at the very back, against the pigment epithelium that nourishes them and absorbs stray light. In front of them, nearer the lens, are the layers of neurons that process their signals. Light therefore travels through the ganglion cell layer, whose axons gather to form the optic nerve; then the inner plexiform layer, where amacrine cells link bipolar and ganglion cells; then the bipolar cells; then the horizontal cells, which spread sideways where receptors meet bipolar cells and produce lateral inhibition that sharpens edges; and finally the rods and cones. Neural signals then flow the opposite way: receptors to bipolar cells to ganglion cells to the brain. This design creates the blind spot, because the ganglion axons must pass back through the retina at the optic disc, where there is no room for receptors. In the fovea the overlying cells are pushed aside, so light reaches its cones more directly. Example: Meera, revising, says 'light meets G, B, H, and the cones come last'. Option d is right because it alone follows the light from the front layer to the back. Exam trap: students often give the direction of the neural signal (receptors first), which is the reverse of the path the light takes.", "topic": "Retinal layers and light path", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The resting potential of a Neuron is", "options": {"a": "-95 mv", "b": "+85 mv", "c": "-70 mv", "d": "+70 mv"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: a typical neuron at rest has a membrane potential of about -70 millivolts, which means the inside is about 70 millivolts more negative than the outside. A resting neuron is said to be polarised. Measured with a fine microelectrode, the value usually lies between about -60 and -70 millivolts (some texts allow down to -80), and -70 is the standard textbook figure. Three things keep it there. First, ions are unevenly spread: sodium (Na+) and chloride (Cl-) are concentrated outside, while potassium (K+) and large negative proteins are concentrated inside. Second, at rest the membrane is far more permeable to potassium, which leaks out through open channels and leaves negative charge behind. Third, the sodium-potassium pump spends energy pushing out three sodium ions for every two potassium ions it brings in. When the neuron is depolarised to threshold, about -55 millivolts, an action potential fires and the inside swings to a peak of about +40 millivolts before returning to rest. Option c is right because -70 millivolts is the textbook resting value. Exam trap: the sign matters. At rest the inside is negative, so +70 and +85 are impossible. And -95 is too negative: the resting value sits a little less negative than the potassium equilibrium potential (the voltage the membrane would reach if only potassium could cross it), because a small leak of sodium into the cell pulls it upward.", "topic": "Resting membrane potential", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The chi-square, computed for a contingency table, was based on six degrees of freedom. If the contingency table had three rows, how many columns would it have?", "options": {"a": "2", "b": "3", "c": "4", "d": "6"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the table must have 4 columns. For a chi-square test on a contingency table, the degrees of freedom (df) equal the number of rows minus 1 multiplied by the number of columns minus 1: df = (r - 1)(c - 1). Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square test, and Ronald Fisher (1922) showed that this is the correct df for a contingency table. Degrees of freedom are the number of cell frequencies that are free to vary once the row and column totals are fixed. In each row, once all but one cell is filled, the last cell is forced by the row total; the same holds for each column. So one row and one column are 'used up'. Here df = 6 and r = 3. So 6 = (3 - 1)(c - 1) = 2(c - 1), which gives c - 1 = 3 and c = 4. Check: a 3 by 4 table gives 2 times 3 = 6. Example: Arjun cross-tabulates three income groups (rows) against four preferred types of therapy (columns); his chi-square has 6 degrees of freedom. Option c is right because only 4 columns satisfies (3 - 1)(c - 1) = 6. Exam trap: students divide 6 by the 3 rows to get 2, or use rows times columns minus 1, or forget to subtract 1 from the rows; always subtract 1 from both before multiplying. A one-way (goodness-of-fit) chi-square, with a single row of k categories, uses k - 1 instead.", "topic": "Chi-square degrees of freedom", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Test-Retest reliability of Psychometric tests is more than that of projective tests. Reason (R): Trait approach presumes consistency of personality.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but the trait assumption in R is not the reason objective tests are more stable over time than projective ones. Test-retest reliability is the correlation between scores when the same test is given twice to the same people; it shows how stable the scores are. Judge A on its own: it is true. Objective psychometric inventories such as the MMPI, the 16PF and the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire usually show retest coefficients of about 0.7 to 0.9. Projective techniques such as the Rorschach inkblots and the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) usually show lower and more variable figures, because ambiguous stimuli invite open responses, scoring depends on the judge, and stories shift with mood and recent events. Judge R on its own: it is also true. The trait approach of Gordon Allport (1937), Raymond Cattell (1946) and Hans Eysenck (1947) assumes that personality consists of fairly stable dispositions that show across time and situations. Does R explain A? No. The higher stability of objective tests comes from how they are built and scored: fixed items, fixed answer choices and a scoring key. Example: Meera takes the 16PF and a set of TAT cards twice, a month apart; her 16PF profile barely changes, while her TAT stories differ because the cards leave so much open. Option b is right because both claims hold but R does not supply the cause of A. Exam trap: R sounds like a reason, since stable traits should give stable scores, but projective testing also assumes a stable personality underneath. Key note: some read R as explaining A, but the psychodynamic theory behind projectives also assumes a lasting personality, so the gap in retest reliability comes from test structure and scoring, not from the trait assumption. The official UGC answer after grievances is b, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Reliability: objective versus projective", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Measure of Intelligence) with List-II (Psychologist): p. EEG, q. Reaction Time, r. Culture Fair Test, s. Verbal Test. 1. Binet, 2. Eysenck, 3. Galton, 4. Horn", "options": {"a": "p-1, q-2, r-3, s-4", "b": "p-3, q-1, r-2, s-4", "c": "p-2, q-3, r-4, s-1", "d": "p-4, q-2, r-1, s-3"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: EEG goes with Hans Eysenck (1982), reaction time with Francis Galton (1884), the culture fair test with John Horn (1966, with Raymond Cattell), and the verbal test with Alfred Binet (1905). Each pairing reflects a different idea about how to measure intelligence. Galton opened his Anthropometric Laboratory in London in 1884 and measured simple reaction time and sensory sharpness, believing that a quick, keen nervous system marks the intelligent person. Binet, with Theodore Simon, took the opposite route in 1905: his scale tested judgement, comprehension and reasoning through largely verbal tasks such as defining words and spotting absurdities. Eysenck, in the book he edited, A Model for Intelligence (1982), argued that intelligence has a biological basis and promoted EEG measures, especially average evoked potentials, as an index of neural efficiency. Horn and Cattell (1966) developed the theory of fluid and crystallised intelligence; fluid intelligence, the ability to reason with new material, is what culture-fair tests aim to measure, because they keep language and school knowledge to a minimum. Example: Kabir sorts the four by asking 'brain waves, stopwatch, pictures without words, word puzzles?'. Option c is right because it is the only code in which every pairing is defensible. Exam trap: Eysenck also studied reaction time and Galton founded mental testing in general, so the wrong codes shuffle names that sound plausible; anchor Binet to the verbal test first, since only code c has s-1. Key note: Eysenck also used reaction time and the Culture Fair Intelligence Test itself is Raymond Cattell's, but c is the only code that gives every name a defensible pairing, and it is the official UGC answer. The answer used here stands.", "topic": "Measures of intelligence and pioneers", "fig": ""}, {"q": "After the recent heavy catastrophic floods in a given state, a psychologist interviewed 120 participants to understand the psychological consequences of the event. This is an example of", "options": {"a": "Ex post facto field research", "b": "Field experiment", "c": "Controlled experiment", "d": "Psychometric research"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: interviewing flood survivors after the disaster is ex post facto field research. 'Ex post facto' is Latin for 'after the fact'. Fred Kerlinger (1964), in Foundations of Behavioral Research, described it as research in which the independent variable has already occurred or cannot be manipulated, so the researcher works backward from the observed effects to their presumed cause. Here the independent variable is the flood. No psychologist can create a flood, or decide at random who is hit and who is spared, so there is no manipulation and no random assignment. What the psychologist can do is go to the affected area afterwards and measure outcomes such as anxiety, sleep problems, grief or post-traumatic stress, perhaps comparing badly hit and lightly hit villages. Because the data are gathered in people's real surroundings rather than a laboratory, it is a field study. The design has known weaknesses: exposed and unexposed groups differ in many ways besides the flood, so causal conclusions are weak, and it is tempting to assume that whatever came first caused whatever followed. Example: Kabir, a clinical psychologist, interviews 120 survivors in Kerala three months after the floods to record their distress. Option a is right because the event came first, the researcher had no control over it, and the work took place in the community. Exam trap: 'field' appears in two options, and what separates them is manipulation. A field experiment still has the researcher deliberately change something, such as assigning some villages to a counselling programme; here nothing was changed.", "topic": "Ex post facto field research", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Parts of the Eye) with List-II (Descriptions): p. Rods and Cones, q. Iris, r. Ciliary muscles, s. Blind spot. 1. Stable and unique in individual and can be used for identification better than finger prints, 2. Photoreceptors in retina, 3. The optic disc where optic nerve fibers leave the eye enroute to the brain, 4. Control its curvature which varies depending on the distance of the object focussed", "options": {"a": "p-4, q-1, r-2, s-3", "b": "p-2, q-1, r-4, s-3", "c": "p-1, q-2, r-3, s-4", "d": "p-3, q-4, r-1, s-2"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: rods and cones go with photoreceptors in the retina, the iris with a stable and unique pattern used for identification, the ciliary muscles with controlling the curvature of the lens, and the blind spot with the optic disc. Light enters through the cornea and the pupil. The iris is the coloured ring of muscle around the pupil; it widens or narrows the pupil to control how much light gets in. Its fine pattern of furrows and crypts forms early in life, stays stable and differs even between identical twins, so iris scanning identifies people more reliably than fingerprints; John Daugman (1993) developed the algorithms behind most iris recognition systems. Behind the pupil sits the lens. The ciliary muscles change its shape in accommodation: to focus on a near object they contract, the suspensory ligaments (zonules) slacken, and the elastic lens rounds up; for distant objects they relax and the lens flattens. At the back of the eye, the retina holds about 120 million rods for dim-light vision and about 6 million cones for colour and fine detail, packed most densely in the fovea. The blind spot is the optic disc, where ganglion cell axons leave the eye as the optic nerve; it has no receptors, so an image falling there is not seen. Example: when Riya looks up from her phone to read a distant bus number, her ciliary muscles relax and her lenses flatten. Option b is right because it gives p-2, q-1, r-4 and s-3, each part matched to its job. Exam trap: the ciliary muscles act on the lens, not on the pupil; changing pupil size is the job of the iris.", "topic": "Parts of the eye", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Visual variables) with List-II (Explanations): p. Colour, q. Illuminance, r. Luminance, s. Brightness. 1. The effect of intensity of light on the observer, 2. The intensity of light reflected from an illuminated surface, 3. The amount of incident light, 4. The subjective or psychological effect that different wavelengths of light have on the observer", "options": {"a": "p-1, q-2, r-3, s-4", "b": "p-4, q-3, r-2, s-1", "c": "p-2, q-1, r-3, s-4", "d": "p-1, q-4, r-2, s-3"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: colour goes with the psychological effect of different wavelengths, illuminance with the amount of incident light, luminance with the intensity of light reflected from an illuminated surface, and brightness with the effect of light intensity on the observer. Vision science separates the physical stimulus from the experience it produces. Two of these terms are physical. Illuminance is the amount of light falling on a surface, measured in lux; switching on a desk lamp raises the illuminance of the page. Luminance is the amount of light coming off a surface toward the eye, reflected or emitted, measured in candelas per square metre; under the same lamp a white page reflects more light than a black one, so it has higher luminance. The other two terms are psychological. Brightness is how intense a light looks to the observer; it depends mainly on luminance but also on adaptation and surroundings, so the same grey patch looks brighter on a black background than on a white one. Colour, or hue, is the experience produced by different wavelengths: about 450 nanometres looks blue, about 550 green and about 650 red. The useful pairs are luminance (physical) with brightness (psychological), and wavelength (physical) with hue (psychological). Example: in a dim classroom Arjun's white notebook has low luminance, yet it still looks white rather than grey, because his brain judges it against its surroundings (lightness constancy). Option b is right because it gives p-4, q-3, r-2 and s-1. Exam trap: illuminance and luminance differ by two letters; illuminance is light arriving at a surface, luminance is light leaving it.", "topic": "Physical and perceived light terms", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Given below are the three types of psychological investigations: (1) Field experiments, (2) Laboratory experiments, and (3) Ex post facto field studies. If the above investigations are arranged in descending order in terms of researcher's ability to control secondary variance, the typical order would be", "options": {"a": "2, 1, 3", "b": "3, 1, 2", "c": "2, 3, 1", "d": "3, 2, 1"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: from most to least control over secondary variance, the order is laboratory experiment (2), field experiment (1), ex post facto field study (3). Fred Kerlinger (1964), in Foundations of Behavioral Research, summed up good design as the MAXMINCON principle: maximise systematic (primary) variance from the independent variable, minimise error variance, and control extraneous (secondary) variance, the variation caused by variables other than the one being studied. Control comes from three tools: manipulation, random assignment and isolation from outside influences. A laboratory experiment has all three: the researcher manipulates the independent variable, assigns participants at random and holds lighting, noise, instructions and timing constant. A field experiment still manipulates and often randomises, but in a real setting such as a school or factory, where many uncontrolled events intrude. An ex post facto field study has neither manipulation nor random assignment: the independent variable (a flood, a divorce, a disability) has already happened, so extraneous variance can be controlled only by matching or by measuring possible confounds and adjusting for them statistically. Realism runs the other way: field and ex post facto studies sit closer to everyday life and so tend to have higher external validity. Example: Meera tests whether music helps memory in a quiet laboratory, then repeats the test in a noisy college canteen, and finally compares students who already happen to study with music; her control drops at each step. Option a is right because it lists the laboratory, then the field experiment, then the ex post facto study. Exam trap: the question asks for descending control; reversing the order (3, 1, 2) ranks the designs by realism instead.", "topic": "Control across research designs", "fig": ""}, {"q": "What is the correct sequence of events for hunger motivation? 1. Energy level to its set point, 2. A bout of eating, 3. Presence of an energy deficit, 4. Satiation, 5. Hunger", "options": {"a": "1, 2, 3, 4, 5", "b": "3, 5, 2, 1, 4", "c": "2, 1, 3, 5, 4", "d": "5, 4, 3, 1, 2"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the set-point sequence is presence of an energy deficit (3), hunger (5), a bout of eating (2), energy level back to its set point (1), and satiation (4). Set-point theories treat hunger like a thermostat. The body has a target level of energy, and when stored or circulating energy falls below it, the deficit is detected and hunger switches on. Hunger starts a meal. Eating restores energy to the set point, and that return produces satiation, which ends the meal until the next deficit. This is the classic set-point assumption about eating, as summarised and criticised by John Pinel and colleagues (2000). The best-known versions are the glucostatic theory of Jean Mayer (1953), in which the regulated level is blood glucose, and the lipostatic theory of Gordon Kennedy (1953), in which it is body fat. Critics point out that people often eat when they are not short of energy, and that a strict set-point system could not explain the worldwide rise in obesity. The main alternative is positive-incentive theory, which says we are drawn to eat mostly by the expected pleasure of food, with body weight drifting to a settling point. Example: Arjun skips lunch, his blood glucose falls (deficit), he feels hungry, he eats a plate of rice and dal, his energy recovers, and he feels full and stops. Option b is right because it is the only order in which the deficit causes hunger, hunger causes eating, and restored energy brings satiation. Exam trap: satiation is the last step, not the moment of eating; it follows the return to the set point.", "topic": "Set-point cycle of hunger", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Persons high in achievement motivation tend to prefer tasks that are _____.", "options": {"a": "very easy", "b": "extremely difficult", "c": "moderately difficult", "d": "all types of task"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: people high in achievement motivation prefer moderately difficult tasks. David McClelland, John Atkinson and colleagues (1953), in The Achievement Motive, defined the need for achievement (nAch) as a learned desire to meet standards of excellence, and measured it by scoring the stories people told to Thematic Apperception Test pictures for achievement themes. John Atkinson (1957) built a risk-taking model to explain task choice. The pull of a task depends on the chance of success and on the pride that success brings, and these trade off: easy tasks usually succeed but give little pride, while very hard tasks give great pride but rarely succeed. The combination is strongest when the chance of success is about 50 percent, so people high in nAch choose tasks of intermediate difficulty, where effort really decides the outcome. People high in fear of failure do the opposite: they pick very easy tasks, where failure is unlikely, or very hard ones, where failure is excusable. In the ring-toss study of John Atkinson and George Litwin (1960), students high in nAch mostly threw from middle distances, while students high in test anxiety more often stood very close to the peg or very far away. Example: Kabir, high in nAch, chooses a project that stretches him but that he can finish with hard work, rather than an easy copy or an impossible task. Option c is right because moderate difficulty gives the best mix of likely success and meaningful accomplishment. Exam trap: 'extremely difficult' sounds like the choice of an ambitious person, but it is the pattern of someone driven by fear of failure.", "topic": "Achievement motivation and task choice", "fig": "David McClelland"}, {"q": "Match List-I (Definition) with List-II (Memory system): p. Memory for factual information that we acquired at a specific time, q. Memory for general, abstract knowledge that we cannot remember acquiring at a specific time, r. Memory for information necessary to perform skilled motor activity, s. Memory for events in our own life. 1. Procedural memory, 2. Episodic memory, 3. Autobiographical memory, 4. Semantic memory", "options": {"a": "p-2, q-4, r-1, s-3", "b": "p-4, q-1, r-3, s-2", "c": "p-3, q-2, r-4, s-1", "d": "p-1, q-3, r-2, s-4"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: memory for factual information acquired at a specific time is episodic memory, general knowledge you cannot remember acquiring is semantic memory, memory for skilled motor activity is procedural memory, and memory for events in your own life is autobiographical memory. Endel Tulving (1972) split long-term memory for facts and events, later called declarative or explicit memory, into two systems. Episodic memory stores particular experiences tagged with a time and a place, such as what you learnt in yesterday's lecture or where you parked. Semantic memory stores general knowledge, word meanings and facts about the world, such as New Delhi being the capital of India, without any memory of when or where you learnt them. Neal Cohen and Larry Squire (1980) contrasted 'knowing that' with 'knowing how': procedural memory holds skills and habits such as cycling or typing, shown in performance rather than recalled. Brenda Milner (1962) found that the amnesic patient H.M. improved at mirror drawing over days while remembering none of the practice. Autobiographical memory is memory for the events and facts of one's own life; Martin Conway and Christopher Pleydell-Pearce (2000) describe it as built from episodic memories organised around the self and life periods. Example: Riya remembers learning about neurons in Tuesday's class (episodic), knows that neurons carry signals (semantic), can still ride the bicycle she learnt on at seven (procedural), and can tell the story of her first year at college (autobiographical). Option a is right because it gives p-2, q-4, r-1 and s-3. Exam trap: semantic and episodic are easily swapped; the clue is whether a time and place are attached. Key note: p and s overlap, since autobiographical memory is built largely from episodic memories; the stem separates them by the words 'our own life'; the official UGC answer after grievances is a, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Long-term memory systems", "fig": "Endel Tulving"}, {"q": "Match List-I (Phenomenon) with List-II (Psychologist): p. Observational learning, q. Cognitive Map, r. Association of different stimuli, s. Learning pulls to cognitive development. 1. Skinner, 2. Vygotsky, 3. Bandura, 4. Pavlov", "options": {"a": "p-2, q-1, r-3, s-4", "b": "p-3, q-1, r-4, s-2", "c": "p-3, q-1, r-2, s-4", "d": "p-2, q-3, r-1, s-4"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: observational learning goes with Albert Bandura (1961), association of different stimuli with Ivan Pavlov (1927), learning that pulls cognitive development with Lev Vygotsky (1978), and the cognitive map is left with B. F. Skinner (1938), the one name remaining in List II. In the Bobo doll studies, Bandura showed that children who watched an adult hit an inflatable doll copied the aggression without being rewarded; his Social Learning Theory (1977) named four steps: attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. Pavlov showed that when a neutral stimulus such as a sound is repeatedly paired with food, a dog comes to salivate to the sound alone: learning by associating two different stimuli, or classical conditioning. Vygotsky, in Mind in Society (1978, English edition), held that good learning runs ahead of development and pulls it along, especially in the zone of proximal development, the gap between what a child can do alone and what she can do with help from a more skilled person. The cognitive map is really the idea of Edward Tolman (1948): rats that explored a maze without reward later ran it as well as rewarded rats once food appeared, showing they had formed a mental picture of its layout. Example: Arjun learns shoelaces by watching his brother (Bandura), flinches at the dentist's drill (Pavlov), and solves harder sums with his teacher's hints (Vygotsky). Option b is right because it is the only code that pairs Bandura, Pavlov and Vygotsky correctly. Exam trap: every wrong code hands 'learning pulls development' to Pavlov; 'learning leads development' is Vygotsky's reversal of the older view that development must come first. Key note: the cognitive map is Tolman's idea, not Skinner's, and Skinner rejected inner maps; since Tolman is not in List II, q-1 is the setter's slip. Code b is the only one that pairs the other three correctly, it is the official UGC answer after grievances, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Learning phenomena and theorists", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): The minimum flicker rate of a light source that results in a perceptual shift from apparent flicker to apparent steady continuous light is known as Critical Flicker Frequency (CFF). Reason (R): The CFF marks the border between seeing flicker and seeing fusion.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "(A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but R only restates the definition in A from the other side, so it does not explain it. A light that flashes on and off slowly is seen to flicker. As the flash rate rises, a point is reached where the flashes blend into a steady, continuous light, which is called fusion. Judge A on its own: it is true. The critical flicker frequency (CFF), also called the critical fusion frequency, is the lowest flash rate at which flicker gives way to apparent steady light. In bright light it is around 50 to 60 flashes per second (hertz), which is why ordinary room lights, which pulse faster than that, look steady. CFF depends on conditions: the Ferry-Porter law states that it rises in proportion to the logarithm of the light's intensity, so it is lower in dim light. Clinically, CFF is used as an index of arousal, fatigue and drug effects, and in detecting early hepatic encephalopathy. Judge R on its own: it is also true. The CFF is indeed the border between seeing flicker and seeing fusion. Does R explain A? No. A says what CFF is; R says the same thing in different words. A definition cannot be its own cause, and R names no mechanism, such as the limited speed at which photoreceptors and visual pathways can follow changes in light. Example: Meera's CFF is measured before and after an all-night study session, and it drops when she is tired, a classic sign of fatigue. Option b is right because both claims are correct but R merely restates A. Exam trap: when R agrees with A, students pick a; always ask whether R gives a cause. Key note: some readers pick a because R agrees with A, but the official UGC answer after grievances is b, because R restates A rather than giving the reason for it. The answer used here stands.", "topic": "Critical flicker frequency", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A person rattles the box of dog's biscuits before giving the biscuit to dog. As the person rattles the box the dog begins to salivate. Thus rattling of box is _____; and the salivation of dog is a kind of _______.", "options": {"a": "CS; CR", "b": "CS; UCR", "c": "UCS; CR", "d": "UCS; UCR"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the rattling of the box is the conditioned stimulus (CS), and the dog's salivation to the rattle is a conditioned response (CR). This is classical conditioning, discovered by Ivan Pavlov (1897 onwards; Conditioned Reflexes, 1927) while he studied digestion in dogs. Classical conditioning has four parts. The unconditioned stimulus (UCS) triggers a response without any learning: here, the biscuit in the dog's mouth. The unconditioned response (UCR) is that unlearned reaction: salivating to the biscuit. A neutral stimulus, the sound of the rattling box, at first produces no salivation. Because the rattle has come just before the biscuit many times, it now predicts food and has become a conditioned stimulus. The salivation it now triggers, before any biscuit arrives, is the conditioned response. Pavlov saw the CS as a substitute for the food; Robert Rescorla (1968) later showed that what matters is that the CS reliably predicts the UCS, not merely that the two occur together. If the box is then rattled many times without biscuits, the CR fades (extinction), and it may return after a rest (spontaneous recovery). Example: Riya's dog Bruno now runs to the kitchen and drools whenever she shakes the treat jar. Option a is right because the rattle is a learned signal and the salivation it causes is a learned response. Exam trap: salivation looks like 'the same response' to both stimuli, so students call it a UCR, but it is labelled by what triggers it: salivation to food is the UCR, salivation to the signal is the CR.", "topic": "Classical conditioning: CS and CR", "fig": "Ivan Pavlov"}, {"q": "In operant conditioning a schedule of reinforcement that reinforces a response at unpredictable time intervals is the case of", "options": {"a": "Fixed-ratio schedule", "b": "Variable-ratio schedule", "c": "Fixed-interval schedule", "d": "Variable-interval schedule"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: reinforcing a response after unpredictable intervals of time is a variable-interval schedule. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957), in Schedules of Reinforcement, studied how the rule for delivering reinforcement shapes the pattern of responding. Schedules differ on two questions: does reinforcement depend on the number of responses (ratio) or on time passing (interval), and is the requirement fixed or variable? On a variable-interval (VI) schedule, the first response after a varying, unpredictable period is reinforced; a VI 5-minute schedule reinforces on average every 5 minutes, sometimes after 1 minute, sometimes after 9. Because a reward could become available at any moment, the organism responds at a steady, moderate rate without long pauses, and the response is quite resistant to extinction. On a fixed-interval schedule the first response after a set time is reinforced, producing a 'scallop': little responding just after reinforcement, then a burst as the time approaches. Ratio schedules give higher rates because more responses bring more reward: a fixed ratio (every nth response, like piece-rate pay) produces a high rate with a pause after each reward, and a variable ratio (an unpredictable number, like a slot machine) produces the highest, steadiest rate and the greatest resistance to extinction. Example: Kabir checks his phone for a reply from a friend who answers at random times; extra checking does not bring the reply sooner, so he checks at a steady, moderate pace. Option d is right because 'unpredictable time intervals' combines variable with interval. Exam trap: 'unpredictable' tempts students toward variable ratio, but ratio means a number of responses; the stem is about time.", "topic": "Schedules of reinforcement", "fig": "Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Learning refers to the modification of behaviour as a result of past experience. Reason (R): Experience is achieved through observation.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "(A) and (R) are correct, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(R) is true, but (A) is false"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but R does not explain A. Learning is usually defined as a relatively permanent change in behaviour, or in the potential for behaviour, that results from experience or practice, a definition that descends from Gregory Kimble (1961). The phrase \"as a result of experience\" rules out changes caused by maturation (a baby starting to walk), fatigue, illness, injury or drugs. Judge A on its own: modification of behaviour through past experience is the textbook core of learning, so A is true. Judge R on its own: we do gain experience by watching others. Albert Bandura (1961) showed in the Bobo doll studies that children who watched an adult hit a doll later copied the attack without ever being rewarded, so observation is a real source of experience, and R is true. Now ask whether R explains A. A says learning comes from experience in general. R names just one route to experience; others are direct practice, trial and error with reward and punishment (Edward Thorndike, 1898), and the pairing of stimuli in classical conditioning. Because experience is far wider than observation, R does not tell us why learning depends on experience. Example: Meera learns to swim by practising in a pool and learns to make chai by watching her grandmother; both are learning, but only the second came from observation. Option b is right because each statement is true on its own, yet R describes one narrow channel of experience rather than the reason behaviour changes with experience. Exam trap: when R repeats a word from A (\"experience\"), students assume a causal link and pick a; always check whether R gives the reason for A or only a related fact.", "topic": "Definition of learning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A person had a blue car that was in the shop more than it was out. Since then the person could not think of owning a blue or green coloured car. The person's aversion even to green cars is an example of", "options": {"a": "Discrimination", "b": "Generalization", "c": "Latent learning", "d": "The overjustification effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: the person's dislike spreading from blue cars to green cars is stimulus generalisation. Once a response has been conditioned to one stimulus, it also appears to other stimuli that resemble it, and the more similar the new stimulus, the stronger the response. This pattern is called the generalisation gradient. Carl Hovland (1937) conditioned a skin response to one tone and found smaller and smaller responses to tones further away in pitch. Norman Guttman and Harry Kalish (1956) trained pigeons to peck a key lit with one wavelength of light and found that pecking fell off steadily as the test colour moved away from it, a gradient built on colour, just like this stem. In the Little Albert study, John Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) conditioned fear of a white rat, and the fear spread to a rabbit, a dog and a fur coat. Here the unreliable blue car (repeated breakdowns, cost, frustration) became linked with unpleasant feelings. Green lies next to blue in the spectrum, so the aversion spread to it. Example: after Arjun's blue scooter kept breaking down, he now feels uneasy even about a teal one in the showroom. Option b is right because the negative reaction learned with one stimulus (blue) has transferred to a similar stimulus (green) that was never part of the bad experience. Exam trap: discrimination is the opposite process; if the person disliked only blue cars and felt fine about green ones, that would be discrimination, so do not pick it just because two colours are mentioned.", "topic": "Stimulus generalisation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the serial position curve?", "options": {"a": "Greater accuracy of recall of words in the beginning of the list and in the middle of the list", "b": "Greater accuracy of recall of words in the beginning of the list and gradual diminished accuracy by the end of the list", "c": "Greater accuracy of recall of words in beginning and at end of the list", "d": "Greater accuracy of recall of words in the middle and at the end of the list"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the serial position curve shows better recall for words at the beginning and at the end of a list than for words in the middle. In a free recall task a person hears a list of perhaps 15 to 20 words and then writes down as many as possible in any order. Plotting the percentage recalled against each word's position gives a U-shaped curve, studied systematically by Bennet Murdock (1962); Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) had noticed position effects much earlier with nonsense syllables. The high start is the primacy effect: the first words get the most rehearsal, so they pass into long-term memory. The high end is the recency effect: the last words are still in short-term memory when recall begins, so they come out first. Words in the middle get little rehearsal and are pushed out of short-term memory, so they are recalled worst. Murray Glanzer and Anita Cunitz (1966) showed that the two ends have different causes: a 30-second counting task before recall wiped out recency but left primacy intact, while slower presentation, which allows more rehearsal, raised primacy. This supports the multi-store model of memory, with separate short-term and long-term stores. Example: Kabir is asked to buy 15 items at the market without a list; he remembers the first few (milk, eggs, bread) and the last few, but forgets the middle ones. Option c is right because it is the only option that names both peaks, primacy and recency. Exam trap: options that pair the beginning with the middle, or the middle with the end, describe a curve that does not exist; the middle is always the trough.", "topic": "Serial position effect", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The degree to which construct system allows new information to enter that will allow construct system to change is called", "options": {"a": "Individual Corollary", "b": "Permeability", "c": "Loose construing", "d": "Constructive Alternativism"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: in George Kelly's (1955, The Psychology of Personal Constructs) theory, permeability is the degree to which a construct admits new elements, so that the construct system can take in new experience and change. Kelly pictured each person as a scientist who builds personal constructs, bipolar dimensions such as friendly versus hostile or safe versus risky, to predict events. His theory has one fundamental postulate (a person's processes are channelled by the ways he or she anticipates events) and eleven corollaries. The modulation corollary says that change in a construct system is limited by the permeability of the constructs within whose range the changes lie. A permeable construct can take in events it has never been applied to before; an impermeable one is closed, so new evidence bounces off it, as in rigid prejudice. Kelly measured constructs with the Role Construct Repertory Test (Rep test), which asks how two people are alike and different from a third. Example: Riya once construed classmates only as \"toppers versus failures\". After joining a theatre group she lets that construct take in new people and new qualities, such as creative and kind, and her whole way of judging people shifts; her construct was permeable. Option b is right because permeability is exactly the property that decides whether new information can enter and revise the construct system. Exam trap: constructive alternativism also sounds like openness to change, but it is Kelly's broad philosophy that any event can be construed in alternative ways, not the property of a particular construct.", "topic": "Kelly's personal construct theory", "fig": "George Kelly"}, {"q": "Who among the following has developed a test for personality assessment where the subject has to choose between a pair of jokes?", "options": {"a": "McCafe", "b": "McCall", "c": "J.M. Cattell", "d": "R.B. Cattell"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: the personality test in which the subject chooses the funnier of a pair of jokes comes from Raymond B. Cattell, the British-American trait theorist. Raymond B. Cattell (1905 to 1998) used factor analysis to find 16 source traits and built the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949). He also believed personality shows in what a person finds funny. Raymond Cattell and Lester Luborsky (1947), in \"Personality factors in response to humor\", factor analysed people's preferences among large sets of jokes and found that humour choices cluster into a few broad factors linked to personality. This line of work became the IPAT Humor Test of Personality, published by Cattell's Institute for Personality and Ability Testing. Each item presents two short jokes and the person marks the one that seems funnier, a forced choice format, and the pattern of choices is scored on humour dimensions that reflect personality. Because the person does not know what is being measured, the test is a disguised, indirect way to assess personality. Example: Arjun keeps choosing jokes built on clever wordplay over jokes about embarrassment; his friend Kabir does the reverse, and on the humour test their choices would load on different factors. Option d is right because R.B. Cattell is the Cattell who studied humour and personality through factor analysis. Exam trap: two famous Cattells appear in exams. James McKeen Cattell (1860 to 1944) coined the term \"mental test\" (1890) and measured reaction time and sensory abilities; he did not build personality tests.", "topic": "Cattell's humour test", "fig": "Raymond B. Cattell"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Working memory can hold a large amount of information even though it can retain only seven to nine items. Reason (R): Items in working memory contain several separate bits of information grouped together called chunks.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are true, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is true, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is true"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and chunking (R) is exactly why a store limited to about seven items can still hold a large amount of information (A). George Miller (1956), in \"The magical number seven, plus or minus two\", showed that immediate memory holds about 7 units, give or take 2, so 5 to 9. Judge A: the limit is set in units, not in raw information. Seven words carry far more information than seven single digits, yet both fill the span, so a store of seven to nine items can hold a lot. A is true. Judge R: a chunk is a meaningful unit formed by grouping separate bits with the help of knowledge already in long-term memory, such as a word, a date or an acronym. William Chase and Herbert Simon (1973) found that chess masters recall whole board positions at a glance because they see familiar patterns of pieces as single chunks. R is true. Does R explain A? Yes: capacity is counted in chunks, and each chunk packs several bits, so the total information held can be large even though the number of items is small. Example: Meera cannot repeat the 12 letters B J P I S R O R B I U N heard once, but regrouped as BJP, ISRO, RBI and UN they become four easy chunks. Option a is right because R gives the very mechanism that makes A possible. Exam trap: \"seven items\" and \"large amount of information\" seem to clash, so students mark A false; the clash disappears once items are understood as chunks. Note too that Miller wrote of short-term memory; the working memory model came later (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974), and Nelson Cowan (2001) puts the limit nearer 4 chunks.", "topic": "Chunking and memory span", "fig": "George Miller"}, {"q": "Who has emphasized the linguistic relativity hypothesis?", "options": {"a": "Noam Chomsky", "b": "Jean Piaget", "c": "Steven Pinker", "d": "Whorf B.L."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: the linguistic relativity hypothesis is tied to Benjamin Lee Whorf (1956, Language, Thought, and Reality, published after his death), who built on the ideas of his teacher Edward Sapir (1929), so it is also called the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. It claims that the language you speak shapes how you think about and perceive the world. It comes in two strengths. The strong form, linguistic determinism, says language fixes thought, so speakers of very different languages live in different mental worlds. The weak form, linguistic relativity proper, says language only nudges what we notice, remember and find easy to think about. Whorf argued, for example, that the Hopi language treats time differently from English, so Hopi speakers think about time differently. Later research challenged the strong form. Eleanor Rosch (1972, 1973) studied the Dani of New Guinea, whose language has only two basic colour terms, and found they still learned and remembered the focal colours more easily than other shades, much as English speakers do. Weaker effects of language on memory and attention do find support. Example: Kabir speaks a language with separate words for a mother's brother (mama) and a father's brother (chacha), so he may notice that family distinction more readily than an English speaker who says only \"uncle\". Option d is right because Whorf is the name most closely tied to the claim that language shapes thought. Exam trap: Noam Chomsky and Steven Pinker are famous language theorists, but both argue for a universal, inborn capacity for language that does not lock thought into a particular language, the reverse of Whorf.", "topic": "Linguistic relativity hypothesis", "fig": "Benjamin Lee Whorf"}, {"q": "Telegraphic speech is typical of the ______ stage.", "options": {"a": "Babbling", "b": "One-word", "c": "Two-word", "d": "Three-word"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: telegraphic speech is typical of the two-word stage, which most children reach at about 18 to 24 months. The name compares toddler speech to an old telegram, where senders paid by the word and kept only the essential ones. Roger Brown (1973, A First Language) described how children at this stage keep content words (nouns, verbs, adjectives) and drop function words such as articles, prepositions and auxiliary verbs, along with endings such as the plural -s or the past tense -ed. So a child says \"want milk\", \"daddy go\" or \"more juice\", yet the word order already follows the adult language, a sign of early grammar. The usual sequence is: cooing (vowel-like sounds, about 2 months), babbling (consonant-vowel strings such as \"bababa\", about 6 months), the one-word or holophrastic stage (about 12 months, a single word doing the work of a sentence), the two-word telegraphic stage (about 18 to 24 months), and then multiword speech with grammatical endings, when overregularisation errors such as \"two foots\" appear. Example: two-year-old Arjun points at the window and says \"car go\"; he means \"the car is going away\" and leaves out every small word. Option c is right because telegraphic speech is defined by these two-word combinations of content words. Exam trap: the holophrase of the one-word stage also carries a whole sentence's meaning, so students confuse the two; a holophrase is one word, while telegraphic speech strings at least two words together. Key note: telegraphic features carry on into early three-word utterances, but textbooks classically tie the term to the two-word stage; it is the official UGC answer after grievances, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Telegraphic speech", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The sentence \"Blue Jeans wear false smiles\" has correct _____ but incorrect _______.", "options": {"a": "Morphemes; phonemes", "b": "Phonemes; morphemes", "c": "Semantics; syntax", "d": "Syntax; semantics"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: \"Blue jeans wear false smiles\" has correct syntax but incorrect semantics. Language is analysed at several levels. Phonemes are the smallest units of sound that change meaning (the /b/ and /p/ that separate \"bat\" from \"pat\"). Morphemes are the smallest units of meaning (\"jean\", the plural \"-s\", \"smile\"). Syntax is the set of rules for combining words into grammatical sentences. Semantics is the study of meaning, of words and of whole sentences. Check the sentence: an adjective and noun as subject (blue jeans), a verb (wear), and an adjective and noun as object (false smiles). That is perfect English word order, so the syntax is correct. But jeans are not living things and cannot wear smiles, so the sentence makes no literal sense; the semantics fails. Noam Chomsky (1957, Syntactic Structures) made the same point with \"Colourless green ideas sleep furiously\": a sentence can be grammatical yet meaningless, which shows that syntax is separate from meaning. Every phoneme and morpheme in the jeans sentence is a real English unit used properly, so those levels are fine. Example: if Riya's little brother says \"Milk the drinks cat\", each word means something but the order breaks the rules, an error of syntax; \"The cat drinks milk\" is correct at both levels. Option d is right because the grammatical form is correct while the meaning is not. Exam trap: option c names the same two terms in reverse order; read carefully which blank is \"correct\" and which is \"incorrect\".", "topic": "Syntax and semantics", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Assertion (A): In deduction, conclusion is derived from a premise. Reason (R): People reason by using deduction as well as induction.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the true explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is correct, but (R) is false", "d": "(A) is false, but (R) is correct"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but R does not explain A. Judge A on its own: deductive reasoning draws a conclusion from one or more premises, and if the premises are true and the argument is valid, the conclusion must be true. The classic case is the syllogism, first set out by Aristotle: all humans are mortal; Socrates is human; so Socrates is mortal. Deduction runs from the general to the particular. A is true. Judge R on its own: inductive reasoning goes the other way, from particular observations to a probable general rule (\"every swan I have seen is white, so all swans are white\"), and its conclusions can be overturned by new evidence. People use both every day. Peter Wason (1966) showed with his four-card selection task that people often fail at formal deduction, and Philip Johnson-Laird (1983, Mental Models) argued that people reason by building mental models of the premises. R is true. Now ask whether R explains A. A states what deduction is. R only reports that people use deduction alongside induction. That fact does not tell us why a deductive conclusion follows from its premises; that comes from the logical form of the argument. So R is true but beside the point. Example: Kabir reasons \"all trains to Pune leave from platform 4; this is the Pune train; so it leaves from platform 4\" (deduction), and also \"the last five Monday trains were late, so Monday trains are usually late\" (induction). Option b is right because both statements hold, but R does not give the reason for A. Exam trap: R mentions deduction, so it looks connected; a shared word is not an explanation.", "topic": "Deductive and inductive reasoning", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Phenomenon) with List-II (Psychological name): p. Smallest unit that carries meaning, q. Rules to communicate and understand others, r. Rules for deriving meanings, s. Rules for combining words. 1. Grammar, 2. Semantics, 3. Morphemes, 4. Syntax", "options": {"a": "p-3, q-1, r-4, s-2", "b": "p-3, q-2, r-4, s-1", "c": "p-3, q-1, r-2, s-4", "d": "p-3, q-2, r-1, s-4"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the smallest unit that carries meaning goes with morphemes (p-3), the rules to communicate with and understand others go with grammar (q-1), the rules for deriving meanings go with semantics (r-2), and the rules for combining words go with syntax (s-4). Language is studied at levels that build on one another. At the bottom are phonemes, the smallest sound units that change meaning (English has about 40). Phonemes combine into morphemes, the smallest units that carry meaning: \"unhappiness\" has three, \"un\", \"happy\" and \"ness\", and the plural \"-s\" is a morpheme too. Grammar is the whole system of rules in a language that lets speakers produce messages and understand others. Within it, semantics is the set of rules for deriving meaning from morphemes, words and sentences, so we know that \"-ed\" signals the past and that \"bank\" means one thing in \"river bank\" and another in \"bank loan\". Syntax is the set of rules for ordering words into sentences, such as subject, verb, object in English. Pragmatics, the social use of language in context, is a further level not listed here. Example: when Meera hears \"The baby is unhappy\", syntax tells her the word order is legal, semantics lets her combine \"un\" and \"happy\" into \"not happy\", and her grammar as a whole lets her understand and reply. Option c is right because it is the only code that gives each of the four phenomena its correct term. Exam trap: semantics and syntax are both \"rules\", so codes swap them; semantics is about meaning, syntax about word order. Every option fixes p-3, so the decision rests on q, r and s.", "topic": "Levels of language structure", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Three types of feature detector cells are", "options": {"a": "Simple cells, Glial cells, T-cells", "b": "Simple cells, Complex cells, Glial cells", "c": "Sensory cells, Hyper complex cells, T-cells", "d": "Simple cells, Complex cells, Hyper complex cells"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the three types of feature detector cells are simple cells, complex cells and hypercomplex cells. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959, 1962) recorded the electrical activity of single neurons in the visual cortex of anaesthetised cats, and later monkeys, while projecting lines and bars onto a screen. They found cells tuned to specific features of the image, so these came to be called feature detectors. Simple cells, in the primary visual (striate) cortex, fire most to a bar or edge of one particular orientation at one particular place in the receptive field; their fields have separate excitatory and inhibitory zones. Complex cells also prefer a particular orientation but respond wherever the bar falls within a larger field, and many prefer movement in one direction. Hypercomplex cells, described by Hubel and Wiesel in 1965 and now usually called end-stopped cells, respond best to a bar of a particular length, or to corners and angles, and fire less when the bar runs past the end of the field. This hierarchy suggested that vision builds complex shapes out of simple features. Hubel and Wiesel shared the 1981 Nobel Prize with Roger Sperry. Example: as Riya reads the letter \"L\", simple cells respond to its vertical and horizontal strokes, complex cells keep responding as her eyes shift slightly, and end-stopped cells signal the corner. Option d is right because it lists the three classic cell types and nothing else. Exam trap: the wrong options mix in real cells from outside this system; glial cells and T-cells exist, but they are not feature detectors, so any list containing them fails.", "topic": "Feature detector cells", "fig": "David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel"}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Sleep fulfills a critical biological function. Reason (R): Sleep is required to restore the homeostasis.", "options": {"a": "Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)", "b": "Both (A) and (R) are correct, but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)", "c": "(A) is false, but (R) is true", "d": "(A) is true, but (R) is false"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: both statements are correct, and R is taken as the explanation of A. Judge A: sleep serves a vital biological function. People kept awake show sleepiness, low mood, lapses of attention, microsleeps of a few seconds, and weaker immune and metabolic function. Allan Rechtschaffen and colleagues (1983) kept rats awake on a rotating disc, and total sleep deprivation killed them within a few weeks. So A is true. Judge R: restorative (recuperation) theories, argued by Ian Oswald (1980) among others, say sleep repairs and rebalances what waking runs down. Alexander Borbely's (1982) two-process model describes a homeostatic sleep pressure (Process S) that builds the longer we stay awake and is discharged during sleep, especially slow-wave sleep, alongside a circadian Process C. Lulu Xie and colleagues (2013) found that the brain clears waste products faster during sleep through the glymphatic system. R is true. Does R explain A? On this reading, yes: sleep is biologically critical because it restores the internal balance (homeostasis) that waking disturbs. Example: after Arjun stays up all night before an exam, his next night has extra deep sleep; the built-up sleep pressure is paid back, a homeostatic pattern. Option a is right because R gives a reason, homeostatic restoration, for why sleep is biologically critical. Exam trap: students often treat \"sleep is necessary\" and \"sleep restores homeostasis\" as two unrelated facts and pick b. Key note: the function of sleep is still debated; the adaptive or evolutionary view (Wilse Webb, 1974; Ray Meddis, 1975) and memory consolidation are rivals to restoration, so some would pick b. The official UGC answer after grievances is a, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Functions of sleep", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Choose the odd one for pattern of sympathetic activation during emotions:", "options": {"a": "Increased heart rate", "b": "Pupil constriction", "c": "Lowered skin resistance", "d": "Increased breathing rate"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: pupil constriction is the odd one out, because it is a parasympathetic response, while the other three are signs of sympathetic activation. The autonomic nervous system has two branches that usually work in opposition. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for action, the fight or flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915). During strong emotions such as fear or anger it raises heart rate and blood pressure, speeds breathing and widens the airways, dilates the pupils (mydriasis) to let in more light, releases glucose for energy, slows digestion and increases sweating. Sweat is salty water that conducts electricity, so sweating lowers skin resistance (raises skin conductance); this galvanic skin response is one of the channels a polygraph records. The parasympathetic branch is the rest and digest system: it slows the heart, stimulates digestion and constricts the pupils (miosis) through fibres in the oculomotor nerve. Example: when a dog suddenly lunges at Meera on the street, her heart pounds, she breathes fast, her palms sweat and her pupils widen; once she is safe at home, parasympathetic activity slows her heart and her pupils return to normal. Option b is right because constriction of the pupil is the parasympathetic opposite of the sympathetic dilation seen in emotion. Exam trap: \"lowered skin resistance\" sounds like a decrease and so like calming, but it reflects more sweating and is a classic sign of sympathetic arousal.", "topic": "Sympathetic arousal in emotion", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Out of following who has not divided intelligence into two general components?", "options": {"a": "Cattell J.M.", "b": "Jensen", "c": "Hebb", "d": "Thurstone"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Louis Thurstone did not divide intelligence into two general components; he proposed several primary abilities. Louis Thurstone (1938, Primary Mental Abilities) gave a large battery of tests to students and analysed them with his method of multiple factor analysis. Instead of one general factor he found about seven primary mental abilities: verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, memory, perceptual speed and reasoning. He later accepted that these abilities correlate, but his model is a multiple-factor one, not a two-part split. Each of the other names proposed a two-way division. Raymond Cattell (1963), later with John Horn (1966), separated fluid intelligence (reasoning with new problems, declining with age) from crystallised intelligence (accumulated knowledge, holding up with age). Arthur Jensen (1969) separated Level I associative ability (rote memory, digit span) from Level II conceptual ability (abstract reasoning, close to g). Donald Hebb (1949, The Organization of Behavior) separated Intelligence A, the brain's innate potential, from Intelligence B, the level of functioning that develops through experience. Example: Riya's skill with a new puzzle and her vocabulary from years of reading are Cattell's two components; her separate scores on number, space and verbal tests are Thurstone's style. Option d is right because Thurstone's model has around seven abilities, not two. Exam trap: Thurstone and Cattell are both factor theorists, so students lump them together; count the components. Key note: the paper prints \"Cattell J.M.\", but the fluid and crystallised split is Raymond B. Cattell's; James McKeen Cattell made no such split, so read option a as R.B. Cattell. Thurstone is the clear answer either way; it is the official UGC answer after grievances, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Two-component theories of intelligence", "fig": "Louis Thurstone"}, {"q": "Number of different factors as specified by Spearman to explain the performance on some tests:", "options": {"a": "One 's' factor and many 'g' factors", "b": "Many 's' factors and many 'g' factors", "c": "One 's' factor and one 'g' factor", "d": "Many 's' factors and one 'g' factor"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: Charles Spearman explained test performance with one general factor, g, and many specific factors, s. Charles Spearman (1904), in \"General intelligence, objectively determined and measured\", noticed that children's scores on very different school subjects and sensory tasks all correlated positively, a pattern now called the positive manifold. To explain it he developed an early form of factor analysis and proposed the two-factor theory, set out fully in The Abilities of Man (1927). Every intellectual task draws on a single general factor, g, which he likened to mental energy, and also on a specific factor, s, unique to that task. Because there is only one g, it explains why all the tests correlate; because each test has its own s, it explains why the correlations are not perfect. The number of s factors therefore equals the number of different tests, so there are many. Reasoning tests built on abstract relations, such as Raven's Progressive Matrices (John Raven, 1938), are among the purest measures of g. Later models added group factors between g and s, as in Philip Vernon's (1950) hierarchical model. Example: Kabir does well in arithmetic, vocabulary and puzzles because of a high g, but his outstanding spelling reflects an extra specific ability, his s for spelling. Option d is right because Spearman's theory has exactly one g and as many s factors as there are tests. Exam trap: the letters get reversed; g is general and single, s is specific and many. Do not confuse Spearman's two kinds of factor with Raymond Cattell's (1963) two kinds of general ability (fluid and crystallised).", "topic": "Spearman's two-factor theory", "fig": "Charles Spearman"}, {"q": "Which set of three approaches is more relevant to study the impact of Genetic factors on intelligence? 1. Correlation between siblings, 2. Correlation between identical twins reared apart, 3. Adopted child studies, 4. Correlation between parents and children", "options": {"a": "1, 2 and 3", "b": "2, 3 and 4", "c": "1, 2 and 4", "d": "1, 3 and 4"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: correlations between siblings, between identical twins reared apart, and between parents and children (1, 2 and 4) form the set taken as most relevant here. Behaviour genetics tests heredity by asking whether people who share more genes are more alike in IQ, the family resemblance or kinship correlation method. Siblings share on average half their varying genes and correlate about 0.45 to 0.5 in IQ. Parents and their children also share half their genes and correlate about 0.4. Identical (monozygotic) twins share all their genes, and if they grow up in different homes their likeness cannot come from a shared upbringing, which makes them a strong test of heredity. Thomas Bouchard and Matt McGue (1981) reviewed 111 studies and found that identical twins reared apart correlate about 0.7, far above unrelated people, and the Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart (Thomas Bouchard and colleagues, 1990) found a similar figure. Resemblance that rises with genetic relatedness is the core evidence that genes influence intelligence. Example: if Arjun and his identical twin, separated at birth and raised in Delhi and Chennai, still score within a few points of each other, that likeness points to their shared genes. Option c is right because all three approaches measure IQ resemblance against degree of genetic relatedness. Exam trap: adoption studies are also a genuine behaviour genetics method, which is why options containing 3 tempt students. Key note: adoption studies, which compare adopted children with their biological and adoptive parents (as in the Texas and Colorado Adoption Projects), are an equally standard method, so this item is debatable. The official UGC answer after grievances is c, which groups the three correlation-by-kinship approaches, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Kinship studies of intelligence", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Psychologist) with List-II (Concept): p. Adler, q. Bandura, r. Jung, s. Horney. 1. Basic anxiety, 2. Triadic Reciprocal causation, 3. Fictional Finalism, 4. Principle of Equivalence", "options": {"a": "p-2, q-4, r-3, s-1", "b": "p-1, q-3, r-2, s-4", "c": "p-3, q-2, r-4, s-1", "d": "p-3, q-4, r-2, s-1"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: Adler goes with fictional finalism (p-3), Bandura with triadic reciprocal causation (q-2), Jung with the principle of equivalence (r-4) and Horney with basic anxiety (s-1). Alfred Adler (1912, The Neurotic Constitution) borrowed from Hans Vaihinger's The Philosophy of 'As If' (1911) the idea that people live by useful fictions. Fictional finalism is an imagined future goal, such as \"I must be respected\", that is never fully real yet pulls behaviour forward. Albert Bandura (1978) called his view reciprocal determinism and later, in Social Foundations of Thought and Action (1986), triadic reciprocal causation: personal factors (beliefs, expectations), behaviour and environment all influence one another in both directions. Carl Jung (1928, On Psychic Energy) treated libido as general psychic energy obeying two principles taken from physics. The principle of equivalence says energy withdrawn from one activity or value does not vanish but reappears in another; the principle of entropy says energy flows from stronger to weaker values, towards balance. Karen Horney (1937, The Neurotic Personality of Our Time) traced neurosis to basic anxiety, the child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, which drives moving toward, against or away from people. Example: when Meera gives up competitive dance and pours the same passion into teaching children, Jung would say the principle of equivalence is at work. Option c is right because it is the only code in which all four pairings are correct. Exam trap: option d keeps Adler and Horney right but trades Bandura's and Jung's concepts; remember that Bandura's three-way causation joins person, behaviour and environment, while equivalence is about energy moving between interests.", "topic": "Personality theorists and concepts", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Researchers have found different ways in which an individual's genotype shapes his or her environment. Which of the following is NOT one of them?", "options": {"a": "The genotype has a passive effect on environment resulting from genetic similarity between parents and children", "b": "The child's genotype evokes particular kinds of reactions from social and physical environment", "c": "The child's genotype plays an active role in shaping the environment", "d": "People with different genotypes are differentially sensitive or susceptible to their environment"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: differential sensitivity of different genotypes to the same environment is not a way in which the genotype shapes the environment; it is gene-environment interaction. The stem is about gene-environment correlation, the finding that genes and environments are not independent because genes partly select or create the environments people meet. Robert Plomin, John DeFries and John Loehlin (1977) named three kinds, and Sandra Scarr and Kathleen McCartney (1983) turned them into a developmental theory. Passive correlation: parents pass on both genes and a home that suits those genes, as when musical parents give a child musical genes and a house full of instruments; it is strongest in infancy. Evocative (reactive) correlation: the child's inherited traits draw particular responses, as a cheerful, smiling baby is picked up and played with more. Active correlation, or niche picking: people seek out settings that suit their genetic leanings, as a sociable teenager joins the drama club; it grows with age as children gain choice. Option d describes something different: two people in the same environment react differently because of their genes. Avshalom Caspi and colleagues (2002) found that maltreated boys with a low-activity MAOA gene variant were more likely to become antisocial than maltreated boys with the high-activity variant. That is the genotype changing the response to the environment, not shaping the environment itself. Example: energetic Kabir keeps asking for football coaching (active), and his coach gives him extra attention because of his enthusiasm (evocative). Option d is right because it is the only statement about sensitivity to the environment rather than about the genotype creating the environment. Exam trap: interaction and correlation sound alike; correlation is about which environments you get, interaction about how you respond to them.", "topic": "Gene-environment correlation", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Match List-I (Types of Variable) with List-II (Example): p. Continuous, q. Real discrete, r. Artificially discrete, s. Qualitative. 1. Selection test outcome in terms of selected/rejected, 2. Voice pitch, 3. Intelligence, 4. Gender", "options": {"a": "p-4, q-1, r-2, s-3", "b": "p-2, q-4, r-1, s-3", "c": "p-3, q-1, r-4, s-2", "d": "p-3, q-4, r-1, s-2"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: intelligence goes with continuous (p-3), gender with real discrete (q-4), the selection outcome of selected or rejected with artificially discrete (r-1), and voice pitch with qualitative (s-2). A continuous variable can take any value along an unbroken scale, so between any two values there is always another; intelligence is treated as a continuous trait even though tests report whole-number scores. A discrete variable comes in separate categories with nothing in between. In a real (true) discrete variable the categories exist in nature, such as gender as classically taught, or alive versus dead. In an artificially discrete variable, a continuum is cut into categories by the researcher: candidates' scores run smoothly, but a cut-off divides them into selected and rejected. This matters for statistics: a true dichotomy correlated with a continuous variable calls for the point-biserial correlation, an artificial dichotomy for the biserial correlation. A qualitative variable is described by kind rather than amount; on this list voice pitch is the item left to be judged by its quality (high, low, husky, shrill). Example: in Riya's recruitment study, aptitude scores are continuous, gender is real discrete, the hire-or-reject decision is artificially discrete, and the interviewer's note of a \"soft, husky voice\" is qualitative. Option d is right because it is the only code that gets the three clear pairings right and leaves voice pitch as the qualitative item. Exam trap: a pass or fail result looks like a natural split, but it is artificial, because the cut-off is placed on a continuous score. Key note: voice pitch is physically continuous (frequency in hertz), so filing it as qualitative only works if pitch is judged as a quality; but d is the only code with the three clear pairings, and it is the official UGC answer after grievances. The answer used here stands.", "topic": "Types of variables", "fig": ""}, {"q": "From the passage (Classical Test Theory: every obtained score is the true score plus error of measurement; split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach alpha, Spearman-Brown, test-retest and interscorer reliability; content, concurrent, predictive and construct validity; factor analysis). Reliability is conceptualized as", "options": {"a": "Obtained variance / True variance", "b": "True variance / (True variance + Error variance)", "c": "True variance / Error variance", "d": "Obtained variance / Error variance"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: in Classical Test Theory, reliability is the proportion of obtained variance that is true variance, that is, true variance divided by (true variance plus error variance). Classical Test Theory, founded by Charles Spearman (1904) and set out in full by Harold Gulliksen (1950, Theory of Mental Tests), starts from one equation: every obtained score X equals a true score T plus a random error E, so X = T + E. Errors are assumed to be random, to average zero over many testings, and to be uncorrelated with true scores. Because T and E are uncorrelated, their variances simply add: obtained variance = true variance + error variance. Reliability is then true variance / obtained variance, which is the same as true variance / (true variance + error variance). It runs from 0 (all error) to 1 (no error). A reliability of 0.80 means 80 percent of the variance in people's scores reflects true differences and 20 percent reflects error. The square root of reliability, the index of reliability, is the correlation between obtained and true scores, the consistency between true and obtained scores that the passage describes. The standard error of measurement follows from it: the standard deviation multiplied by the square root of (1 minus r). Example: on Arjun's anxiety scale, if true variance is 40 and error variance is 10, reliability is 40 / 50 = 0.80. Option b is right because it puts true variance on top and the full obtained variance (true plus error) underneath. Exam trap: option a turns the ratio upside down, which would give values of 1 or more; a reliability coefficient can never exceed 1.", "topic": "Reliability in Classical Test Theory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "From the passage (Classical Test Theory: every obtained score is the true score plus error of measurement; split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach alpha, Spearman-Brown, test-retest and interscorer reliability; content, concurrent, predictive and construct validity; factor analysis). Cronbach alpha is: 1. larger than split half reliability coefficient, 2. smaller than reliability coefficient, 3. average of all possible split-half reliability coefficients, 4. equal to Kuder-Richardson reliability for dichotomous items", "options": {"a": "1 only", "b": "2 only", "c": "3 and 4 only", "d": "1 and 4 only"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is c: statements 3 and 4 are correct; Cronbach's alpha is the average of all possible split-half reliability coefficients, and for items scored right or wrong it equals the Kuder-Richardson reliability (KR-20). Lee Cronbach (1951), in \"Coefficient alpha and the internal structure of tests\", generalised earlier internal consistency formulas. A split-half reliability divides a test into two halves (for example odd versus even items) and correlates them, but the result depends on which split you choose. Cronbach showed that alpha equals the mean of the coefficients from every possible split (each computed by the Rulon or Flanagan formula), which removes that arbitrariness. Alpha is computed from item variances: alpha = [k / (k minus 1)] multiplied by [1 minus (sum of item variances / total test variance)]. When items are scored 0 or 1, each item variance is p times q, and the formula becomes formula 20 of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937). So alpha works for any item format, including Likert ratings, while KR-20 is its special case for dichotomous items. Statement 1 is false: alpha is an average, so some split-halves are larger than it and some smaller. Example: on Meera's 20-item true-false quiz, KR-20 and alpha give the same value, 0.78. Option c is right because only 3 and 4 are the standard textbook properties of alpha. Exam trap: students remember that alpha is \"conservative\" and pick 2, or think an average must exceed its parts and pick 1. Key note: alpha is a lower bound to true reliability, so some read statement 2 as partly true, and the split-half equality holds exactly only for Rulon-type split-halves; but 3 and 4 are the textbook properties and no option pairs 2 with them. It is the official UGC answer after grievances, and the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Cronbach's alpha", "fig": "Lee Cronbach"}, {"q": "From the passage (Classical Test Theory: every obtained score is the true score plus error of measurement; split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach alpha, Spearman-Brown, test-retest and interscorer reliability; content, concurrent, predictive and construct validity; factor analysis). The interscorer reliability is especially important for: 1. Projective tests, 2. Achievement tests with short answers, 3. Multiple choice tests, 4. Judgements by multiple raters", "options": {"a": "1 only", "b": "2 and 3 only", "c": "1 and 4 only", "d": "1, 2 and 4 only"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: interscorer reliability matters most for projective tests, short-answer achievement tests and judgements by multiple raters (1, 2 and 4), but not for multiple-choice tests. Interscorer (inter-rater) reliability is the degree to which two or more people who score the same responses independently arrive at the same results. It is estimated by correlating their scores, by percentage agreement, by Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960), which corrects agreement for chance, or by the intraclass correlation. It becomes a concern whenever scoring needs human judgement. In projective tests, scoring involves interpretation: two psychologists may code the same Rorschach response differently, or read different themes into a Thematic Apperception Test story, which is why the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) trained scorers and reported their agreement. In short-answer and essay achievement tests, examiners must decide how much credit a partly correct answer deserves, so marks can vary across scorers. When several raters judge behaviour, as in classroom observation, interviews or performance appraisal, their agreement must be checked before the ratings can be trusted. A multiple-choice test, by contrast, is scored against a fixed answer sheet, by hand or by machine, so any two scorers get the same total and scorer error is close to zero. Example: two teachers mark Kabir's short-answer history paper and give him 14 and 18 out of 20, a sign of poor interscorer reliability; on his multiple-choice science test both get 17. Option d is right because it lists exactly the three situations in which judgement enters scoring. Exam trap: multiple-choice tests are also \"tests\", so students tick 3 too; objective scoring removes judgement, so interscorer reliability is not an issue there.", "topic": "Interscorer reliability", "fig": ""}, {"q": "From the passage (Classical Test Theory: every obtained score is the true score plus error of measurement; split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach alpha, Spearman-Brown, test-retest and interscorer reliability; content, concurrent, predictive and construct validity; factor analysis). Consider that a newly developed test for assessing abstract reasoning and a well established test of abstract reasoning are administered to a sample of 350 and a correlation of 0.25, significant at .01 level is obtained. It will be inferred that the new test has", "options": {"a": "unsatisfactory concurrent validity", "b": "satisfactory concurrent validity", "c": "unsatisfactory predictive validity", "d": "satisfactory predictive validity"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the new test has unsatisfactory concurrent validity. Concurrent validity is a kind of criterion-related validity in which a test is correlated with a criterion measured at about the same time, here an established test of abstract reasoning. Because both tests are given to the same sample together, this is concurrent, not predictive, validity; predictive validity would need a criterion measured later, such as exam results a year on. The real question is whether r = 0.25 is good enough. It is significant at the .01 level mainly because the sample is large (N = 350); with that many people even a small correlation is unlikely to be zero by chance. But significance says only that some relationship exists, not that it is strong. Squaring the correlation gives the coefficient of determination: 0.25 multiplied by 0.25 is 0.0625, so the two tests share only about 6 percent of their variance. Two tests that claim to measure the same ability should overlap heavily; as a rule of thumb, a new test should correlate around 0.7 or more with an established test of the same construct. A test that agrees so weakly with an accepted measure cannot be trusted to measure abstract reasoning. Example: Riya's new reasoning test and an established reasoning test rank her students quite differently, even though the correlation is \"significant\", so she must revise her items. Option a is right because the criterion was measured at the same time and the correlation is far too small to support the new test. Exam trap: statistical significance is not the same as practical size; a significant r of 0.25 is still weak.", "topic": "Concurrent validity", "fig": ""}, {"q": "From the passage (Classical Test Theory: every obtained score is the true score plus error of measurement; split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach alpha, Spearman-Brown, test-retest and interscorer reliability; content, concurrent, predictive and construct validity; factor analysis). Factor analysis is best suited for studying", "options": {"a": "Concurrent validity", "b": "Predictive Validity", "c": "Content validity", "d": "Construct validity"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: factor analysis is best suited for studying construct validity. Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) in \"Construct validity in psychological tests\", asks whether a test really measures the theoretical trait (construct) it claims to measure, such as anxiety or abstract reasoning. Because constructs cannot be observed directly, the evidence comes from many sources: correlations with related and unrelated measures, group differences, changes after experiments, and the internal structure of the test. Factor analysis, pioneered by Charles Spearman (1904) and extended by Louis Thurstone (1947, Multiple-Factor Analysis), takes a table of correlations among items or tests and finds a smaller number of underlying factors that account for them. If the items of a test meant to measure one construct load strongly on one factor, and that factor links to other tests of the same construct but not to tests of unrelated ones, construct validity is supported. The correlation of a test with a factor is called its factorial validity, which Anne Anastasi's textbook Psychological Testing treats as evidence of construct validity. Example: Arjun builds a 30-item self-esteem scale; factor analysis shows all items loading on one factor, and that factor correlates with an established self-esteem measure, supporting the claim that his scale measures self-esteem. Option d is right because factor analysis examines the internal structure and underlying dimensions of a test, which is the heart of construct validation. Exam trap: concurrent and predictive validity also use correlations, but each correlates the test with a single outside criterion; factor analysis looks at the pattern of many correlations to reveal the construct beneath them.", "topic": "Factor analysis and construct validity", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"ugc-net-2013-dec": [{"q": "The visible spectrum wavelength ranges from", "concept": "The answer is a: the visible spectrum, the band of electromagnetic energy the human eye can detect, runs from about 380 to 760 nanometres. A nanometre is one billionth of a metre. Light is only a thin slice of the electromagnetic spectrum, which also includes gamma rays, X-rays, microwaves and radio waves. Within the visible band, wavelength is experienced as hue: the shortest visible waves (about 380 to 490 nanometres) look violet and blue, the middle ones green and yellow, and the longest (about 620 to 760 nanometres) red. Just below the short end lies ultraviolet, which bees can see but we cannot; just beyond the long end lies infrared, which we feel as heat rather than see. Isaac Newton, in prism experiments he began in 1666, showed that white light is a mixture of these colours by spreading sunlight into a band and then recombining it. Textbooks round the limits differently, giving 380 or 400 at the short end and 700 or 760 at the long end, but every version keeps both ends between ultraviolet and infrared. Riya remembers it as 'roughly 400 to 700, VIBGYOR from short to long'. Option a is right because 380 to 760 nanometres is the standard textbook range for human vision. Exam trap: each wrong option contains a real stretch of visible light but extends it into invisible regions, so check both ends of the range, not just one.", "traps": {"b": "200 to 700 nanometres has an acceptable upper end, but its lower end reaches deep into ultraviolet (below about 380 nanometres), which the human eye cannot see.", "c": "400 to 1000 nanometres starts correctly but runs far into the infrared; wavelengths beyond about 760 nanometres are felt as heat, not seen as light.", "d": "100 to 1000 nanometres overshoots at both ends, taking in ultraviolet and infrared radiation that lie outside human vision."}}, {"q": "Shyam does not like to be in family gatherings but his mother has asked him to attend it. He decides to tell a lie that he has examination on that day however he finds it very distasteful. Shyam is experiencing _____.", "concept": "The answer is a: Shyam is in a double avoidance (avoidance-avoidance) conflict, because both of his choices are unpleasant to him. Kurt Lewin (1935), in A Dynamic Theory of Personality, described conflict as being caught between forces in a person's life space. Goals that attract have positive valence; goals that repel have negative valence. In an avoidance-avoidance conflict a person must pick one of two evils. Shyam dislikes family gatherings, so attending is one negative goal. Escaping with a lie about an examination is the other, and he finds lying distasteful. Neal Miller (1944) showed with rats that the urge to avoid a goal grows steeply the closer one gets to it, so a person in this conflict vacillates: as Shyam moves toward attending, attending feels worse and he swings toward the lie; as he moves toward lying, the lie feels worse and he swings back. Typical outcomes are dithering, delay and 'leaving the field', for instance falling ill on the day or refusing to decide. Option a is right because neither option attracts Shyam; he is trying to avoid both. Exam trap: the lie looks like an escape route, so students call this approach-avoidance, but the lie is itself a second thing Shyam wants to avoid, not an attractive goal with a drawback. For contrast: approach-approach means two attractive goals, approach-avoidance means one goal with good and bad sides, and double approach-avoidance means two options that each have good and bad sides.", "traps": {"b": "Double approach (approach-approach) conflict is a choice between two attractive goals, such as two good job offers; Shyam finds neither of his options attractive.", "c": "Approach-avoidance conflict involves one goal that both attracts and repels, like wanting a sweet but fearing weight gain; Shyam's two options both repel him.", "d": "Double approach-avoidance conflict involves two options that each have clear good and bad sides; the stem gives Shyam nothing he wants in either the gathering or the lie."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Computational approach involves a rigorous, mathematically oriented analysis of certain aspects of visual perception derived largely from computer simulation and artificial intelligence. Reason (R): Neurophysiological approach argues that sensory and perceptual phenomena are best explained by known neural and physiological mechanisms serving sensory structures.", "concept": "The answer is b: both statements are true descriptions of two different approaches to perception, but the second does not explain the first. Perception textbooks list several approaches side by side: psychophysical, Gestalt, constructivist (Hermann von Helmholtz, 1867; Richard Gregory, 1970), direct or ecological (James Gibson, 1979), neurophysiological and computational. Judge A on its own: it is true. The computational approach, set out by David Marr (1982) in his book Vision, treats seeing as information processing that can be analysed mathematically, often by writing computer programs that do the same job. Marr asked three questions about any visual task: what is computed and why (computational level), by what step-by-step procedure (algorithmic level), and in what hardware (implementational level). He traced vision from a primal sketch of edges, through a 2.5-D sketch of surfaces seen from the viewer's position, to a 3-D model of the object. Judge R on its own: it is also true. The neurophysiological approach explains perception through known neural mechanisms, such as the feature detector cells found by David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959, 1962) or lateral inhibition in the retina. Now ask whether R explains A. It does not: R describes a separate school, not the reason the computational approach is mathematical. Arjun, writing an essay on perception, would put them under two headings, not link them as cause and effect. Option b is right because both statements are correct but simply sit side by side. Exam trap: when A and R both describe approaches from the same chapter, students assume a link; ask 'does R tell me why A is true?' before choosing a.", "traps": {"a": "This needs R to give the reason A is true; the neurophysiological approach is a separate school, so it cannot explain why the computational approach is mathematical and computer-based.", "c": "This calls R false, but explaining perception through known neural and physiological mechanisms is exactly what the neurophysiological approach does, as in David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel's feature detectors.", "d": "This calls A false, but A accurately summarises the computational approach associated with David Marr, built on mathematics, computer simulation and artificial intelligence."}}, {"q": "Having seen or heard a stimulus once may facilitate our recognizing it on a later occasion, even if we are not aware that this is happening. This phenomenon is referred to as", "concept": "The answer is d: the priming effect is the unconscious ease in recognising or processing a stimulus because you met it earlier. In a typical study, people read a list of words such as ELEPHANT and later fill in word fragments such as E_E_H_N_. They complete fragments of words they saw earlier more often, even when they cannot say which words were on the list. Endel Tulving, Daniel Schacter and Heather Stark (1982) showed that this fragment-completion priming is independent of recognition memory. Daniel Schacter (1987) called this kind of retention implicit memory: memory shown in performance, not in conscious recollection. Repetition priming involves the same item; semantic priming involves a related one, so seeing BREAD speeds recognition of BUTTER. Strong evidence that priming is separate from conscious memory comes from amnesia. Elizabeth Warrington and Lawrence Weiskrantz (1970) found that amnesic patients who could not recall a list still benefited normally from earlier exposure when given word fragments or first letters as cues. Example: Meera hears a new song playing in a shop without paying attention, and the next day it seems oddly familiar and easy to sing along to. Option d is right because the stem stresses two features, easier later recognition and no awareness, and together those define priming. Exam trap: primacy and recency also involve earlier exposure, but they describe where an item sat in a list that is being recalled consciously, not unconscious facilitation.", "traps": {"a": "The primacy effect is better recall of the first items in a list, thought to reflect extra rehearsal into long-term memory (Bennet Murdock's serial position curve); it concerns conscious recall, not unaware facilitation.", "b": "The recency effect is better recall of the last items in a list because they are still in short-term memory; it is a serial position effect, not an implicit one.", "c": "The practice effect is improvement on a retest because a person is familiar with the test itself; it is general to the task and usually conscious, not the unaware facilitation of one specific stimulus."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Naturalistic observation is the highly preferred method in ethological research. Reason (R): Naturalistic observation is free from observer-bias.", "concept": "The answer is c: naturalistic observation is indeed the preferred method of ethology, but it is not free from observer bias. Ethology is the biological study of animal behaviour in the animal's natural setting. Its founders, Konrad Lorenz, Niko Tinbergen and Karl von Frisch, shared the 1973 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for work such as imprinting in geese, fixed action patterns in sticklebacks and the waggle dance of honeybees. Judge A on its own: it is true. Ethologists want to see behaviour as it evolved to work, in the animal's own habitat, so they watch without interfering. Judge R on its own: it is false. Naturalistic observation is especially open to observer bias, where the observer's expectations shape what is noticed, recorded and interpreted. It also suffers from reactivity (animals or people behave differently when they know they are watched) and from anthropomorphism, reading human motives into animals. Researchers reduce bias with ethograms (clear lists of behaviour categories), video recordings, observers who do not know the hypothesis, and checks of interobserver agreement using Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960). Because R is false, the question of whether it explains A does not arise. Example: Kabir, studying langur monkeys, expects the males to be aggressive and so codes rough play as fighting, until a second coder who does not know his hypothesis disagrees. Option c is right because A is a true statement and R is a false one. Exam trap: 'natural' sounds objective, but no observation is free of bias; bias is reduced by method, not removed by the setting.", "traps": {"a": "This needs both statements to be true, but R is false: naturalistic observation is open to observer bias, so freedom from bias cannot be the reason ethologists prefer it.", "b": "This also needs R to be true; because observers' expectations can colour what they record in the field, R fails before the question of explanation is reached.", "d": "This calls A false, but observing animals in their own habitat is the hallmark of ethology, from Konrad Lorenz's geese to Niko Tinbergen's sticklebacks."}}, {"q": "There are various kinds of cells in human body. Which one of the following is not a neuron?", "concept": "The answer is b: glia are not neurons; they are the support cells of the nervous system. Rudolf Virchow coined the word neuroglia, 'nerve glue', in 1856, because he thought these cells simply held neurons together. Glial cells do not carry action potentials in the way neurons do, but they do far more than hold things in place. Astrocytes, star-shaped cells, supply nutrients to neurons, help form the blood-brain barrier and mop up excess neurotransmitter. Oligodendrocytes wrap myelin around axons in the central nervous system, and Schwann cells do the same in the peripheral nervous system. Microglia act as the brain's immune cleaners, removing debris and dead cells. Neurons themselves are classified by function into three types. Sensory (afferent) neurons carry information from receptors toward the brain and spinal cord. Motor (efferent) neurons carry commands from the central nervous system to muscles and glands. Interneurons (association neurons) lie entirely within the brain and spinal cord, link sensory to motor neurons and are by far the most numerous neurons. Example: when Meera pulls her hand away from a hot pan, a sensory neuron carries the signal to the spinal cord, an interneuron relays it, and a motor neuron makes the arm muscles contract, while glia around all three supply and insulate them. Option b is right because glia are a separate class of cell, while sensory, inter and motor are the three functional types of neuron. Exam trap: glia sit beside neurons everywhere and are roughly as numerous, so they are easily mistaken for a neuron type; remember 'glue', not 'messenger'.", "traps": {"a": "Sensory (afferent) neurons are a genuine neuron type that carries signals from receptors toward the brain and spinal cord, so they cannot be the odd one out.", "c": "Interneurons (association neurons) are genuine neurons that link sensory and motor neurons inside the central nervous system; they are the most numerous type of neuron.", "d": "Motor (efferent) neurons are genuine neurons that carry commands from the central nervous system out to muscles and glands."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): During action potential, there is change in polarization of ions across neuronal membrane. Reason (R): All the negative ions are transported outside the neuronal membrane.", "concept": "The answer is c: A is true, because an action potential is a brief reversal of polarisation across the neuron's membrane, but R is false, because negative ions are not all transported outside. At rest the inside of a neuron is about -70 millivolts relative to the outside. Judge A on its own. When the membrane is depolarised to threshold (about -55 millivolts), voltage-gated sodium channels open and positive sodium ions (Na+) rush in, driving the inside up to about +40 millivolts. The sodium channels then close, and voltage-gated potassium channels let positive potassium ions (K+) flow out, so the membrane repolarises and briefly dips below rest (hyperpolarisation). Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked this out in the giant axon of the squid and shared the 1963 Nobel Prize. So the change in polarisation is real, and it is driven by the movement of positive ions. Judge R on its own. The large negative protein anions inside the cell cannot cross the membrane and stay inside throughout. The sodium-potassium pump moves positive ions (three Na+ out for every two K+ in), not negative ones. Chloride, the main small negative ion, is already concentrated outside. So R is false, and the question of whether it explains A does not arise. Example: Arjun remembers it as 'sodium in, potassium out, proteins stay put'. Option c is right because A is correct and R misdescribes the ions involved. Exam trap: R uses the right vocabulary (ions, membrane, transport) but names the wrong charge, so it sounds plausible while being false.", "traps": {"a": "This needs R to be true, but the action potential is driven by positive sodium ions entering and positive potassium ions leaving; the negative proteins stay inside, so R explains nothing.", "b": "This also treats R as true; because negative ions are not pumped out of the neuron, R fails before any question of explanation arises.", "d": "This calls A false, yet the swing from about -70 to about +40 millivolts and back is exactly the change in polarisation that defines an action potential."}}, {"q": "Individual differences in intelligence are due to", "concept": "The answer is d: individual differences in intelligence arise from many factors, both genetic and environmental, acting together. Behaviour genetics estimates the genetic share with twin and adoption studies. Identical twins, who share all their genes, have more similar IQ scores than fraternal twins, who share about half, and adopted children come to resemble their biological parents more than their adoptive parents as they grow. Heritability, the proportion of differences within a population linked to genetic differences, rises with age: a combined analysis of about 11,000 twin pairs led by Claire Haworth and Robert Plomin (2010) found about 0.4 at age 9 rising to about 0.66 by age 17. But heritability is a population statistic, not a fixed limit for any one person. The environment matters too: prenatal health, nutrition, schooling, a stimulating home, family income and freedom from toxins such as lead. James Flynn (1984, 1987) showed that average IQ scores rose by about three points per decade across many nations, far too fast for genes to explain. Genes and environment also work together: a curious child seeks out books, and the books then sharpen her abilities. Example: identical twins Riya and Meera are adopted into different homes; their scores stay close, yet the twin given better schooling scores a few points higher. Option d is right because it is the only option that includes both genetic and environmental causes without narrowing them. Exam trap: option c sounds balanced, but economic status is only one environmental factor; 'many factors including genetic and environmental' is the full interactionist answer.", "traps": {"a": "'Only genetic factors' is the extreme hereditarian position; twin studies show substantial heritability, but well below 1, and the Flynn effect shows that environment shifts scores too.", "b": "'Only environmental factors' is the extreme environmentalist position; it ignores twin and adoption evidence that genetic relatedness predicts similarity in IQ.", "c": "Genetic and economic factors leaves out most of the environment, such as nutrition, schooling and home stimulation; economic status is only one environmental influence."}}, {"q": "Put the parts of the Brain in sequence, going from higher to lower level of brain: I. Metencephalon, II. Mesencephalon, III. Diencephalon, IV. Telencephalon, V. Myelencephalon", "concept": "The answer is c: from higher to lower, the order is telencephalon (IV), diencephalon (III), mesencephalon (II), metencephalon (I) and myelencephalon (V). These names come from embryology. Early in development the front end of the neural tube swells into three primary vesicles: the forebrain (prosencephalon), midbrain (mesencephalon) and hindbrain (rhombencephalon). The forebrain then splits into the telencephalon and diencephalon, and the hindbrain into the metencephalon and myelencephalon, giving five divisions stacked from the top of the brain down to the spinal cord. The telencephalon becomes the cerebral hemispheres: the cortex, basal ganglia and limbic structures. The diencephalon ('between brain') becomes the thalamus and hypothalamus. The mesencephalon stays as the midbrain, with the tectum (superior and inferior colliculi), the tegmentum and the substantia nigra. The metencephalon becomes the pons and cerebellum. The myelencephalon ('marrow brain') becomes the medulla oblongata, which joins the spinal cord. 'Higher' here means further from the spinal cord and more involved in complex functions such as thought and language; 'lower' means closer to the spinal cord and more concerned with basic life functions such as breathing and heart rate. Example: Kabir notices that once tel- is placed on top, the other four names happen to fall in alphabetical order (di-, mes-, met-, myel-). Option c is right because it is the only sequence that runs from the cerebral hemispheres down to the medulla. Exam trap: mesencephalon and metencephalon differ by a single letter; mes- is the midbrain, and met- is the pons and cerebellum below it.", "traps": {"a": "I, II, III, IV, V starts at the metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) and puts the telencephalon fourth, mixing the hindbrain into the top of the order.", "b": "II, IV, I, III, V starts with the midbrain and places the diencephalon below the metencephalon, so both forebrain divisions are out of place.", "d": "III, II, I, V, IV keeps the right order for the middle four but drops the telencephalon (the cerebral hemispheres, the highest division) to the bottom."}}, {"q": "Arrange the following layers of Retina in order of light travelling from environment to back of the eyeball: I. Ganglion cells, II. Cone receptors, III. Horizontal cells, IV. Bipolar cells", "concept": "The answer is d: light entering the eye passes through the ganglion cells (I) first, then the bipolar cells (IV), then the horizontal cells (III), and only then reaches the cone receptors (II). The vertebrate retina is built 'inside out'. The photoreceptors, rods and cones, lie at the very back, against the pigment epithelium that nourishes them and absorbs stray light. In front of them, nearer the lens, are the layers of neurons that process their signals. Light therefore travels through the ganglion cell layer, whose axons gather to form the optic nerve; then the inner plexiform layer, where amacrine cells link bipolar and ganglion cells; then the bipolar cells; then the horizontal cells, which spread sideways where receptors meet bipolar cells and produce lateral inhibition that sharpens edges; and finally the rods and cones. Neural signals then flow the opposite way: receptors to bipolar cells to ganglion cells to the brain. This design creates the blind spot, because the ganglion axons must pass back through the retina at the optic disc, where there is no room for receptors. In the fovea the overlying cells are pushed aside, so light reaches its cones more directly. Example: Meera, revising, says 'light meets G, B, H, and the cones come last'. Option d is right because it alone follows the light from the front layer to the back. Exam trap: students often give the direction of the neural signal (receptors first), which is the reverse of the path the light takes.", "traps": {"a": "I, II, III, IV puts the cones second, straight after the ganglion cells, but the bipolar and horizontal cells lie between them, so light reaches the receptors last, not second.", "b": "II, III, IV, I begins with the cones and ends with the ganglion cells, which roughly follows the neural signal, the reverse of the path light takes.", "c": "IV, II, III, I starts with the bipolar cells and ends with the ganglion cells; the ganglion cells form the innermost layer, the first one light meets."}}, {"q": "The resting potential of a Neuron is", "concept": "The answer is c: a typical neuron at rest has a membrane potential of about -70 millivolts, which means the inside is about 70 millivolts more negative than the outside. A resting neuron is said to be polarised. Measured with a fine microelectrode, the value usually lies between about -60 and -70 millivolts (some texts allow down to -80), and -70 is the standard textbook figure. Three things keep it there. First, ions are unevenly spread: sodium (Na+) and chloride (Cl-) are concentrated outside, while potassium (K+) and large negative proteins are concentrated inside. Second, at rest the membrane is far more permeable to potassium, which leaks out through open channels and leaves negative charge behind. Third, the sodium-potassium pump spends energy pushing out three sodium ions for every two potassium ions it brings in. When the neuron is depolarised to threshold, about -55 millivolts, an action potential fires and the inside swings to a peak of about +40 millivolts before returning to rest. Option c is right because -70 millivolts is the textbook resting value. Exam trap: the sign matters. At rest the inside is negative, so +70 and +85 are impossible. And -95 is too negative: the resting value sits a little less negative than the potassium equilibrium potential (the voltage the membrane would reach if only potassium could cross it), because a small leak of sodium into the cell pulls it upward.", "traps": {"a": "-95 millivolts is more negative than a resting neuron sits; only a membrane permeable to potassium alone would approach such values, and the small sodium leak keeps the real resting value near -70.", "b": "+85 millivolts has the wrong sign; the inside of a resting neuron is negative, and even the peak of an action potential reaches only about +40 millivolts.", "d": "+70 millivolts reverses the sign of the true value; at rest the inside of the neuron is negative relative to the outside."}}, {"q": "The chi-square, computed for a contingency table, was based on six degrees of freedom. If the contingency table had three rows, how many columns would it have?", "concept": "The answer is c: the table must have 4 columns. For a chi-square test on a contingency table, the degrees of freedom (df) equal the number of rows minus 1 multiplied by the number of columns minus 1: df = (r - 1)(c - 1). Karl Pearson (1900) introduced the chi-square test, and Ronald Fisher (1922) showed that this is the correct df for a contingency table. Degrees of freedom are the number of cell frequencies that are free to vary once the row and column totals are fixed. In each row, once all but one cell is filled, the last cell is forced by the row total; the same holds for each column. So one row and one column are 'used up'. Here df = 6 and r = 3. So 6 = (3 - 1)(c - 1) = 2(c - 1), which gives c - 1 = 3 and c = 4. Check: a 3 by 4 table gives 2 times 3 = 6. Example: Arjun cross-tabulates three income groups (rows) against four preferred types of therapy (columns); his chi-square has 6 degrees of freedom. Option c is right because only 4 columns satisfies (3 - 1)(c - 1) = 6. Exam trap: students divide 6 by the 3 rows to get 2, or use rows times columns minus 1, or forget to subtract 1 from the rows; always subtract 1 from both before multiplying. A one-way (goodness-of-fit) chi-square, with a single row of k categories, uses k - 1 instead.", "traps": {"a": "2 comes from dividing 6 by the 3 rows without subtracting 1; a 3 by 2 table has only (3 - 1)(2 - 1) = 2 degrees of freedom.", "b": "3 is the value of c - 1, not c; a 3 by 3 table gives (2)(2) = 4 degrees of freedom, not 6.", "d": "6 confuses the degrees of freedom with the number of columns; a 3 by 6 table would give (2)(5) = 10 degrees of freedom."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Test-Retest reliability of Psychometric tests is more than that of projective tests. Reason (R): Trait approach presumes consistency of personality.", "concept": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but the trait assumption in R is not the reason objective tests are more stable over time than projective ones. Test-retest reliability is the correlation between scores when the same test is given twice to the same people; it shows how stable the scores are. Judge A on its own: it is true. Objective psychometric inventories such as the MMPI, the 16PF and the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire usually show retest coefficients of about 0.7 to 0.9. Projective techniques such as the Rorschach inkblots and the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) usually show lower and more variable figures, because ambiguous stimuli invite open responses, scoring depends on the judge, and stories shift with mood and recent events. Judge R on its own: it is also true. The trait approach of Gordon Allport (1937), Raymond Cattell (1946) and Hans Eysenck (1947) assumes that personality consists of fairly stable dispositions that show across time and situations. Does R explain A? No. The higher stability of objective tests comes from how they are built and scored: fixed items, fixed answer choices and a scoring key. Example: Meera takes the 16PF and a set of TAT cards twice, a month apart; her 16PF profile barely changes, while her TAT stories differ because the cards leave so much open. Option b is right because both claims hold but R does not supply the cause of A. Exam trap: R sounds like a reason, since stable traits should give stable scores, but projective testing also assumes a stable personality underneath. Key note: some read R as explaining A, but the psychodynamic theory behind projectives also assumes a lasting personality, so the gap in retest reliability comes from test structure and scoring, not from the trait assumption. The official UGC answer after grievances is b, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "This treats the trait assumption as the cause, but projective tests also assume a stable personality (psychodynamic theory); the gap in retest reliability comes from structured items and objective scoring.", "c": "This calls R false, but the trait approach of Gordon Allport, Raymond Cattell and Hans Eysenck does presume that personality is consistent across time and situations.", "d": "This calls A false, but objective inventories such as the MMPI and 16PF typically show higher test-retest coefficients than the Rorschach or TAT."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Measure of Intelligence) with List-II (Psychologist): p. EEG, q. Reaction Time, r. Culture Fair Test, s. Verbal Test. 1. Binet, 2. Eysenck, 3. Galton, 4. Horn", "concept": "The answer is c: EEG goes with Hans Eysenck (1982), reaction time with Francis Galton (1884), the culture fair test with John Horn (1966, with Raymond Cattell), and the verbal test with Alfred Binet (1905). Each pairing reflects a different idea about how to measure intelligence. Galton opened his Anthropometric Laboratory in London in 1884 and measured simple reaction time and sensory sharpness, believing that a quick, keen nervous system marks the intelligent person. Binet, with Theodore Simon, took the opposite route in 1905: his scale tested judgement, comprehension and reasoning through largely verbal tasks such as defining words and spotting absurdities. Eysenck, in the book he edited, A Model for Intelligence (1982), argued that intelligence has a biological basis and promoted EEG measures, especially average evoked potentials, as an index of neural efficiency. Horn and Cattell (1966) developed the theory of fluid and crystallised intelligence; fluid intelligence, the ability to reason with new material, is what culture-fair tests aim to measure, because they keep language and school knowledge to a minimum. Example: Kabir sorts the four by asking 'brain waves, stopwatch, pictures without words, word puzzles?'. Option c is right because it is the only code in which every pairing is defensible. Exam trap: Eysenck also studied reaction time and Galton founded mental testing in general, so the wrong codes shuffle names that sound plausible; anchor Binet to the verbal test first, since only code c has s-1. Key note: Eysenck also used reaction time and the Culture Fair Intelligence Test itself is Raymond Cattell's, but c is the only code that gives every name a defensible pairing, and it is the official UGC answer. The answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "p-1, q-2, r-3, s-4 links EEG to Alfred Binet and the verbal test to John Horn; Binet's scale was a verbal test of reasoning and never used brain recordings.", "b": "p-3, q-1, r-2, s-4 gives EEG to Francis Galton and reaction time to Alfred Binet; Galton measured reaction time, and the human EEG was first recorded by Hans Berger only in the 1920s.", "d": "p-4, q-2, r-1, s-3 pairs the culture fair test with Alfred Binet and the verbal test with Francis Galton; Binet's tests were heavily verbal, while Galton tested sensory and motor speed."}}, {"q": "After the recent heavy catastrophic floods in a given state, a psychologist interviewed 120 participants to understand the psychological consequences of the event. This is an example of", "concept": "The answer is a: interviewing flood survivors after the disaster is ex post facto field research. 'Ex post facto' is Latin for 'after the fact'. Fred Kerlinger (1964), in Foundations of Behavioral Research, described it as research in which the independent variable has already occurred or cannot be manipulated, so the researcher works backward from the observed effects to their presumed cause. Here the independent variable is the flood. No psychologist can create a flood, or decide at random who is hit and who is spared, so there is no manipulation and no random assignment. What the psychologist can do is go to the affected area afterwards and measure outcomes such as anxiety, sleep problems, grief or post-traumatic stress, perhaps comparing badly hit and lightly hit villages. Because the data are gathered in people's real surroundings rather than a laboratory, it is a field study. The design has known weaknesses: exposed and unexposed groups differ in many ways besides the flood, so causal conclusions are weak, and it is tempting to assume that whatever came first caused whatever followed. Example: Kabir, a clinical psychologist, interviews 120 survivors in Kerala three months after the floods to record their distress. Option a is right because the event came first, the researcher had no control over it, and the work took place in the community. Exam trap: 'field' appears in two options, and what separates them is manipulation. A field experiment still has the researcher deliberately change something, such as assigning some villages to a counselling programme; here nothing was changed.", "traps": {"b": "A field experiment manipulates an independent variable in a real-life setting (for example, giving some flood-hit villages a counselling programme and not others); here the psychologist manipulated nothing.", "c": "A controlled (laboratory) experiment manipulates the independent variable under tight control with random assignment; a flood can be neither created nor assigned.", "d": "Psychometric research is about constructing, standardising and validating tests; this psychologist studies the effects of an event, not the properties of a measure."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Parts of the Eye) with List-II (Descriptions): p. Rods and Cones, q. Iris, r. Ciliary muscles, s. Blind spot. 1. Stable and unique in individual and can be used for identification better than finger prints, 2. Photoreceptors in retina, 3. The optic disc where optic nerve fibers leave the eye enroute to the brain, 4. Control its curvature which varies depending on the distance of the object focussed", "concept": "The answer is b: rods and cones go with photoreceptors in the retina, the iris with a stable and unique pattern used for identification, the ciliary muscles with controlling the curvature of the lens, and the blind spot with the optic disc. Light enters through the cornea and the pupil. The iris is the coloured ring of muscle around the pupil; it widens or narrows the pupil to control how much light gets in. Its fine pattern of furrows and crypts forms early in life, stays stable and differs even between identical twins, so iris scanning identifies people more reliably than fingerprints; John Daugman (1993) developed the algorithms behind most iris recognition systems. Behind the pupil sits the lens. The ciliary muscles change its shape in accommodation: to focus on a near object they contract, the suspensory ligaments (zonules) slacken, and the elastic lens rounds up; for distant objects they relax and the lens flattens. At the back of the eye, the retina holds about 120 million rods for dim-light vision and about 6 million cones for colour and fine detail, packed most densely in the fovea. The blind spot is the optic disc, where ganglion cell axons leave the eye as the optic nerve; it has no receptors, so an image falling there is not seen. Example: when Riya looks up from her phone to read a distant bus number, her ciliary muscles relax and her lenses flatten. Option b is right because it gives p-2, q-1, r-4 and s-3, each part matched to its job. Exam trap: the ciliary muscles act on the lens, not on the pupil; changing pupil size is the job of the iris.", "traps": {"a": "p-4, q-1, r-2, s-3 gives rods and cones the job of changing lens curvature and calls the ciliary muscles photoreceptors; the ciliary muscles focus the lens, and rods and cones detect light.", "c": "p-1, q-2, r-3, s-4 gets all four wrong: it gives the iris's identification role to rods and cones, calls the iris a photoreceptor, puts the ciliary muscles at the optic disc and has the blind spot focusing the lens.", "d": "p-3, q-4, r-1, s-2 calls rods and cones the optic disc and the iris a focusing muscle; the optic disc is exactly where there are no rods or cones, and the iris controls pupil size, not lens curvature."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Visual variables) with List-II (Explanations): p. Colour, q. Illuminance, r. Luminance, s. Brightness. 1. The effect of intensity of light on the observer, 2. The intensity of light reflected from an illuminated surface, 3. The amount of incident light, 4. The subjective or psychological effect that different wavelengths of light have on the observer", "concept": "The answer is b: colour goes with the psychological effect of different wavelengths, illuminance with the amount of incident light, luminance with the intensity of light reflected from an illuminated surface, and brightness with the effect of light intensity on the observer. Vision science separates the physical stimulus from the experience it produces. Two of these terms are physical. Illuminance is the amount of light falling on a surface, measured in lux; switching on a desk lamp raises the illuminance of the page. Luminance is the amount of light coming off a surface toward the eye, reflected or emitted, measured in candelas per square metre; under the same lamp a white page reflects more light than a black one, so it has higher luminance. The other two terms are psychological. Brightness is how intense a light looks to the observer; it depends mainly on luminance but also on adaptation and surroundings, so the same grey patch looks brighter on a black background than on a white one. Colour, or hue, is the experience produced by different wavelengths: about 450 nanometres looks blue, about 550 green and about 650 red. The useful pairs are luminance (physical) with brightness (psychological), and wavelength (physical) with hue (psychological). Example: in a dim classroom Arjun's white notebook has low luminance, yet it still looks white rather than grey, because his brain judges it against its surroundings (lightness constancy). Option b is right because it gives p-4, q-3, r-2 and s-1. Exam trap: illuminance and luminance differ by two letters; illuminance is light arriving at a surface, luminance is light leaving it.", "traps": {"a": "p-1, q-2, r-3, s-4 reverses the whole list: it calls colour an effect of intensity, swaps illuminance and luminance, and gives brightness the wavelength definition that belongs to hue.", "c": "p-2, q-1, r-3, s-4 makes colour a reflected intensity and illuminance a perceived effect; illuminance is purely physical, the light falling on a surface.", "d": "p-1, q-4, r-2, s-3 gets luminance right but gives illuminance the definition of hue and brightness the definition of incident light; brightness is an experience, not a physical amount."}}, {"q": "Given below are the three types of psychological investigations: (1) Field experiments, (2) Laboratory experiments, and (3) Ex post facto field studies. If the above investigations are arranged in descending order in terms of researcher's ability to control secondary variance, the typical order would be", "concept": "The answer is a: from most to least control over secondary variance, the order is laboratory experiment (2), field experiment (1), ex post facto field study (3). Fred Kerlinger (1964), in Foundations of Behavioral Research, summed up good design as the MAXMINCON principle: maximise systematic (primary) variance from the independent variable, minimise error variance, and control extraneous (secondary) variance, the variation caused by variables other than the one being studied. Control comes from three tools: manipulation, random assignment and isolation from outside influences. A laboratory experiment has all three: the researcher manipulates the independent variable, assigns participants at random and holds lighting, noise, instructions and timing constant. A field experiment still manipulates and often randomises, but in a real setting such as a school or factory, where many uncontrolled events intrude. An ex post facto field study has neither manipulation nor random assignment: the independent variable (a flood, a divorce, a disability) has already happened, so extraneous variance can be controlled only by matching or by measuring possible confounds and adjusting for them statistically. Realism runs the other way: field and ex post facto studies sit closer to everyday life and so tend to have higher external validity. Example: Meera tests whether music helps memory in a quiet laboratory, then repeats the test in a noisy college canteen, and finally compares students who already happen to study with music; her control drops at each step. Option a is right because it lists the laboratory, then the field experiment, then the ex post facto study. Exam trap: the question asks for descending control; reversing the order (3, 1, 2) ranks the designs by realism instead.", "traps": {"b": "3, 1, 2 is the exact reverse, the order of increasing control (or decreasing realism); it puts the ex post facto study, with no manipulation or random assignment, at the top.", "c": "2, 3, 1 rightly starts with the laboratory but ranks the ex post facto study above the field experiment; a field experiment still manipulates the independent variable, so it controls more.", "d": "3, 2, 1 puts the ex post facto study first and the field experiment last, ranking the design with no manipulation at all as the most controlled."}}, {"q": "What is the correct sequence of events for hunger motivation? 1. Energy level to its set point, 2. A bout of eating, 3. Presence of an energy deficit, 4. Satiation, 5. Hunger", "concept": "The answer is b: the set-point sequence is presence of an energy deficit (3), hunger (5), a bout of eating (2), energy level back to its set point (1), and satiation (4). Set-point theories treat hunger like a thermostat. The body has a target level of energy, and when stored or circulating energy falls below it, the deficit is detected and hunger switches on. Hunger starts a meal. Eating restores energy to the set point, and that return produces satiation, which ends the meal until the next deficit. This is the classic set-point assumption about eating, as summarised and criticised by John Pinel and colleagues (2000). The best-known versions are the glucostatic theory of Jean Mayer (1953), in which the regulated level is blood glucose, and the lipostatic theory of Gordon Kennedy (1953), in which it is body fat. Critics point out that people often eat when they are not short of energy, and that a strict set-point system could not explain the worldwide rise in obesity. The main alternative is positive-incentive theory, which says we are drawn to eat mostly by the expected pleasure of food, with body weight drifting to a settling point. Example: Arjun skips lunch, his blood glucose falls (deficit), he feels hungry, he eats a plate of rice and dal, his energy recovers, and he feels full and stops. Option b is right because it is the only order in which the deficit causes hunger, hunger causes eating, and restored energy brings satiation. Exam trap: satiation is the last step, not the moment of eating; it follows the return to the set point.", "traps": {"a": "1, 2, 3, 4, 5 starts with energy already at its set point and ends with hunger, so eating comes before any deficit and hunger arrives after satiation.", "c": "2, 1, 3, 5, 4 begins with a bout of eating and places the energy deficit after energy has been restored, which leaves the first meal without a cause.", "d": "5, 4, 3, 1, 2 puts satiation straight after hunger and eating last; in the set-point cycle satiation follows eating, it does not come before it."}}, {"q": "Persons high in achievement motivation tend to prefer tasks that are _____.", "concept": "The answer is c: people high in achievement motivation prefer moderately difficult tasks. David McClelland, John Atkinson and colleagues (1953), in The Achievement Motive, defined the need for achievement (nAch) as a learned desire to meet standards of excellence, and measured it by scoring the stories people told to Thematic Apperception Test pictures for achievement themes. John Atkinson (1957) built a risk-taking model to explain task choice. The pull of a task depends on the chance of success and on the pride that success brings, and these trade off: easy tasks usually succeed but give little pride, while very hard tasks give great pride but rarely succeed. The combination is strongest when the chance of success is about 50 percent, so people high in nAch choose tasks of intermediate difficulty, where effort really decides the outcome. People high in fear of failure do the opposite: they pick very easy tasks, where failure is unlikely, or very hard ones, where failure is excusable. In the ring-toss study of John Atkinson and George Litwin (1960), students high in nAch mostly threw from middle distances, while students high in test anxiety more often stood very close to the peg or very far away. Example: Kabir, high in nAch, chooses a project that stretches him but that he can finish with hard work, rather than an easy copy or an impossible task. Option c is right because moderate difficulty gives the best mix of likely success and meaningful accomplishment. Exam trap: 'extremely difficult' sounds like the choice of an ambitious person, but it is the pattern of someone driven by fear of failure.", "traps": {"a": "Very easy tasks are chosen by people high in fear of failure, because success is almost guaranteed; for a high achiever they offer too little sense of accomplishment.", "b": "Extremely difficult tasks are also typical of people high in fear of failure, because failing at an impossible task is easy to excuse; the chance of success is too low to attract a high achiever.", "d": "'All types of task' ignores the central finding of John Atkinson's risk-taking model, that people high in nAch show a clear preference for intermediate difficulty."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Definition) with List-II (Memory system): p. Memory for factual information that we acquired at a specific time, q. Memory for general, abstract knowledge that we cannot remember acquiring at a specific time, r. Memory for information necessary to perform skilled motor activity, s. Memory for events in our own life. 1. Procedural memory, 2. Episodic memory, 3. Autobiographical memory, 4. Semantic memory", "concept": "The answer is a: memory for factual information acquired at a specific time is episodic memory, general knowledge you cannot remember acquiring is semantic memory, memory for skilled motor activity is procedural memory, and memory for events in your own life is autobiographical memory. Endel Tulving (1972) split long-term memory for facts and events, later called declarative or explicit memory, into two systems. Episodic memory stores particular experiences tagged with a time and a place, such as what you learnt in yesterday's lecture or where you parked. Semantic memory stores general knowledge, word meanings and facts about the world, such as New Delhi being the capital of India, without any memory of when or where you learnt them. Neal Cohen and Larry Squire (1980) contrasted 'knowing that' with 'knowing how': procedural memory holds skills and habits such as cycling or typing, shown in performance rather than recalled. Brenda Milner (1962) found that the amnesic patient H.M. improved at mirror drawing over days while remembering none of the practice. Autobiographical memory is memory for the events and facts of one's own life; Martin Conway and Christopher Pleydell-Pearce (2000) describe it as built from episodic memories organised around the self and life periods. Example: Riya remembers learning about neurons in Tuesday's class (episodic), knows that neurons carry signals (semantic), can still ride the bicycle she learnt on at seven (procedural), and can tell the story of her first year at college (autobiographical). Option a is right because it gives p-2, q-4, r-1 and s-3. Exam trap: semantic and episodic are easily swapped; the clue is whether a time and place are attached. Key note: p and s overlap, since autobiographical memory is built largely from episodic memories; the stem separates them by the words 'our own life'; the official UGC answer after grievances is a, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "p-4, q-1, r-3, s-2 calls time-tagged facts semantic and general knowledge procedural; procedural memory is for skills, not abstract knowledge, and semantic memory carries no time tag.", "c": "p-3, q-2, r-4, s-1 makes general knowledge episodic and motor skill semantic; Endel Tulving's episodic memory is defined by a time and place, which general knowledge lacks.", "d": "p-1, q-3, r-2, s-4 gives the skill system to time-tagged facts and calls memory of one's own life semantic; skills belong to procedural memory, and personal events are autobiographical."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Phenomenon) with List-II (Psychologist): p. Observational learning, q. Cognitive Map, r. Association of different stimuli, s. Learning pulls to cognitive development. 1. Skinner, 2. Vygotsky, 3. Bandura, 4. Pavlov", "concept": "The answer is b: observational learning goes with Albert Bandura (1961), association of different stimuli with Ivan Pavlov (1927), learning that pulls cognitive development with Lev Vygotsky (1978), and the cognitive map is left with B. F. Skinner (1938), the one name remaining in List II. In the Bobo doll studies, Bandura showed that children who watched an adult hit an inflatable doll copied the aggression without being rewarded; his Social Learning Theory (1977) named four steps: attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. Pavlov showed that when a neutral stimulus such as a sound is repeatedly paired with food, a dog comes to salivate to the sound alone: learning by associating two different stimuli, or classical conditioning. Vygotsky, in Mind in Society (1978, English edition), held that good learning runs ahead of development and pulls it along, especially in the zone of proximal development, the gap between what a child can do alone and what she can do with help from a more skilled person. The cognitive map is really the idea of Edward Tolman (1948): rats that explored a maze without reward later ran it as well as rewarded rats once food appeared, showing they had formed a mental picture of its layout. Example: Arjun learns shoelaces by watching his brother (Bandura), flinches at the dentist's drill (Pavlov), and solves harder sums with his teacher's hints (Vygotsky). Option b is right because it is the only code that pairs Bandura, Pavlov and Vygotsky correctly. Exam trap: every wrong code hands 'learning pulls development' to Pavlov; 'learning leads development' is Vygotsky's reversal of the older view that development must come first. Key note: the cognitive map is Tolman's idea, not Skinner's, and Skinner rejected inner maps; since Tolman is not in List II, q-1 is the setter's slip. Code b is the only one that pairs the other three correctly, it is the official UGC answer after grievances, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "p-2, q-1, r-3, s-4 gives observational learning to Lev Vygotsky and stimulus association to Albert Bandura; Bandura's work is observational learning and Ivan Pavlov's is stimulus association.", "c": "p-3, q-1, r-2, s-4 gets Albert Bandura right but swaps the other two: association of stimuli is Ivan Pavlov's classical conditioning, and learning leading development is Lev Vygotsky's idea.", "d": "p-2, q-3, r-1, s-4 credits observational learning to Lev Vygotsky, the cognitive map to Albert Bandura and stimulus association to B. F. Skinner, whose work was on the consequences of responses, not paired stimuli."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): The minimum flicker rate of a light source that results in a perceptual shift from apparent flicker to apparent steady continuous light is known as Critical Flicker Frequency (CFF). Reason (R): The CFF marks the border between seeing flicker and seeing fusion.", "concept": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but R only restates the definition in A from the other side, so it does not explain it. A light that flashes on and off slowly is seen to flicker. As the flash rate rises, a point is reached where the flashes blend into a steady, continuous light, which is called fusion. Judge A on its own: it is true. The critical flicker frequency (CFF), also called the critical fusion frequency, is the lowest flash rate at which flicker gives way to apparent steady light. In bright light it is around 50 to 60 flashes per second (hertz), which is why ordinary room lights, which pulse faster than that, look steady. CFF depends on conditions: the Ferry-Porter law states that it rises in proportion to the logarithm of the light's intensity, so it is lower in dim light. Clinically, CFF is used as an index of arousal, fatigue and drug effects, and in detecting early hepatic encephalopathy. Judge R on its own: it is also true. The CFF is indeed the border between seeing flicker and seeing fusion. Does R explain A? No. A says what CFF is; R says the same thing in different words. A definition cannot be its own cause, and R names no mechanism, such as the limited speed at which photoreceptors and visual pathways can follow changes in light. Example: Meera's CFF is measured before and after an all-night study session, and it drops when she is tired, a classic sign of fatigue. Option b is right because both claims are correct but R merely restates A. Exam trap: when R agrees with A, students pick a; always ask whether R gives a cause. Key note: some readers pick a because R agrees with A, but the official UGC answer after grievances is b, because R restates A rather than giving the reason for it. The answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "This would need R to give a cause; R only repeats A's definition (flicker turning into fusion), so it describes CFF again rather than explaining it.", "c": "This calls R false, but the CFF really is the boundary between perceived flicker and perceived fusion; R is accurate.", "d": "This calls A false, but A gives the standard definition of critical flicker frequency: the minimum flash rate at which flicker becomes steady light."}}, {"q": "A person rattles the box of dog's biscuits before giving the biscuit to dog. As the person rattles the box the dog begins to salivate. Thus rattling of box is _____; and the salivation of dog is a kind of _______.", "concept": "The answer is a: the rattling of the box is the conditioned stimulus (CS), and the dog's salivation to the rattle is a conditioned response (CR). This is classical conditioning, discovered by Ivan Pavlov (1897 onwards; Conditioned Reflexes, 1927) while he studied digestion in dogs. Classical conditioning has four parts. The unconditioned stimulus (UCS) triggers a response without any learning: here, the biscuit in the dog's mouth. The unconditioned response (UCR) is that unlearned reaction: salivating to the biscuit. A neutral stimulus, the sound of the rattling box, at first produces no salivation. Because the rattle has come just before the biscuit many times, it now predicts food and has become a conditioned stimulus. The salivation it now triggers, before any biscuit arrives, is the conditioned response. Pavlov saw the CS as a substitute for the food; Robert Rescorla (1968) later showed that what matters is that the CS reliably predicts the UCS, not merely that the two occur together. If the box is then rattled many times without biscuits, the CR fades (extinction), and it may return after a rest (spontaneous recovery). Example: Riya's dog Bruno now runs to the kitchen and drools whenever she shakes the treat jar. Option a is right because the rattle is a learned signal and the salivation it causes is a learned response. Exam trap: salivation looks like 'the same response' to both stimuli, so students call it a UCR, but it is labelled by what triggers it: salivation to food is the UCR, salivation to the signal is the CR.", "traps": {"b": "CS; UCR names the rattle correctly but calls salivation to the rattle unconditioned; salivation is a UCR only when the food itself triggers it.", "c": "UCS; CR calls the rattle an unconditioned stimulus, but a rattling box has no natural power to make a dog salivate; it gained that power only by preceding the biscuit.", "d": "UCS; UCR describes the biscuit and salivation to the biscuit, the unlearned reflex, not the learned response to the rattle described in the stem."}}, {"q": "In operant conditioning a schedule of reinforcement that reinforces a response at unpredictable time intervals is the case of", "concept": "The answer is d: reinforcing a response after unpredictable intervals of time is a variable-interval schedule. Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner (1957), in Schedules of Reinforcement, studied how the rule for delivering reinforcement shapes the pattern of responding. Schedules differ on two questions: does reinforcement depend on the number of responses (ratio) or on time passing (interval), and is the requirement fixed or variable? On a variable-interval (VI) schedule, the first response after a varying, unpredictable period is reinforced; a VI 5-minute schedule reinforces on average every 5 minutes, sometimes after 1 minute, sometimes after 9. Because a reward could become available at any moment, the organism responds at a steady, moderate rate without long pauses, and the response is quite resistant to extinction. On a fixed-interval schedule the first response after a set time is reinforced, producing a 'scallop': little responding just after reinforcement, then a burst as the time approaches. Ratio schedules give higher rates because more responses bring more reward: a fixed ratio (every nth response, like piece-rate pay) produces a high rate with a pause after each reward, and a variable ratio (an unpredictable number, like a slot machine) produces the highest, steadiest rate and the greatest resistance to extinction. Example: Kabir checks his phone for a reply from a friend who answers at random times; extra checking does not bring the reply sooner, so he checks at a steady, moderate pace. Option d is right because 'unpredictable time intervals' combines variable with interval. Exam trap: 'unpredictable' tempts students toward variable ratio, but ratio means a number of responses; the stem is about time.", "traps": {"a": "A fixed-ratio schedule reinforces after a set number of responses (for example every tenth), like piece-rate pay; it depends on the count of responses, not on time.", "b": "A variable-ratio schedule reinforces after an unpredictable number of responses, like a slot machine; it is unpredictable, but in responses, not in time.", "c": "A fixed-interval schedule reinforces the first response after a set, predictable time, producing a scalloped pattern; the stem specifies unpredictable intervals."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Learning refers to the modification of behaviour as a result of past experience. Reason (R): Experience is achieved through observation.", "concept": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but R does not explain A. Learning is usually defined as a relatively permanent change in behaviour, or in the potential for behaviour, that results from experience or practice, a definition that descends from Gregory Kimble (1961). The phrase \"as a result of experience\" rules out changes caused by maturation (a baby starting to walk), fatigue, illness, injury or drugs. Judge A on its own: modification of behaviour through past experience is the textbook core of learning, so A is true. Judge R on its own: we do gain experience by watching others. Albert Bandura (1961) showed in the Bobo doll studies that children who watched an adult hit a doll later copied the attack without ever being rewarded, so observation is a real source of experience, and R is true. Now ask whether R explains A. A says learning comes from experience in general. R names just one route to experience; others are direct practice, trial and error with reward and punishment (Edward Thorndike, 1898), and the pairing of stimuli in classical conditioning. Because experience is far wider than observation, R does not tell us why learning depends on experience. Example: Meera learns to swim by practising in a pool and learns to make chai by watching her grandmother; both are learning, but only the second came from observation. Option b is right because each statement is true on its own, yet R describes one narrow channel of experience rather than the reason behaviour changes with experience. Exam trap: when R repeats a word from A (\"experience\"), students assume a causal link and pick a; always check whether R gives the reason for A or only a related fact.", "traps": {"a": "This needs R to give the reason for A, but observation is only one way of gaining experience (practice, reinforcement and conditioning are others), so it cannot explain the general definition of learning.", "c": "This calls R false, but Albert Bandura's observational learning research shows that watching others is a genuine source of experience, so R is true.", "d": "This calls A false, but a relatively permanent change in behaviour due to experience is the standard definition of learning, so A is true."}}, {"q": "A person had a blue car that was in the shop more than it was out. Since then the person could not think of owning a blue or green coloured car. The person's aversion even to green cars is an example of", "concept": "The answer is b: the person's dislike spreading from blue cars to green cars is stimulus generalisation. Once a response has been conditioned to one stimulus, it also appears to other stimuli that resemble it, and the more similar the new stimulus, the stronger the response. This pattern is called the generalisation gradient. Carl Hovland (1937) conditioned a skin response to one tone and found smaller and smaller responses to tones further away in pitch. Norman Guttman and Harry Kalish (1956) trained pigeons to peck a key lit with one wavelength of light and found that pecking fell off steadily as the test colour moved away from it, a gradient built on colour, just like this stem. In the Little Albert study, John Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) conditioned fear of a white rat, and the fear spread to a rabbit, a dog and a fur coat. Here the unreliable blue car (repeated breakdowns, cost, frustration) became linked with unpleasant feelings. Green lies next to blue in the spectrum, so the aversion spread to it. Example: after Arjun's blue scooter kept breaking down, he now feels uneasy even about a teal one in the showroom. Option b is right because the negative reaction learned with one stimulus (blue) has transferred to a similar stimulus (green) that was never part of the bad experience. Exam trap: discrimination is the opposite process; if the person disliked only blue cars and felt fine about green ones, that would be discrimination, so do not pick it just because two colours are mentioned.", "traps": {"a": "Discrimination is learning to respond to the conditioned stimulus but not to similar ones; it would mean disliking only blue cars and accepting green ones, the reverse of what happened.", "c": "Latent learning (Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik, 1930) is learning that stays hidden until a reward gives a reason to show it, as with rats that learned a maze without food; nothing here is hidden learning.", "d": "The overjustification effect (Mark Lepper, David Greene and Richard Nisbett, 1973) is the loss of intrinsic interest when people are rewarded for an activity they already enjoy; it has nothing to do with an aversion spreading across colours."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the serial position curve?", "concept": "The answer is c: the serial position curve shows better recall for words at the beginning and at the end of a list than for words in the middle. In a free recall task a person hears a list of perhaps 15 to 20 words and then writes down as many as possible in any order. Plotting the percentage recalled against each word's position gives a U-shaped curve, studied systematically by Bennet Murdock (1962); Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885) had noticed position effects much earlier with nonsense syllables. The high start is the primacy effect: the first words get the most rehearsal, so they pass into long-term memory. The high end is the recency effect: the last words are still in short-term memory when recall begins, so they come out first. Words in the middle get little rehearsal and are pushed out of short-term memory, so they are recalled worst. Murray Glanzer and Anita Cunitz (1966) showed that the two ends have different causes: a 30-second counting task before recall wiped out recency but left primacy intact, while slower presentation, which allows more rehearsal, raised primacy. This supports the multi-store model of memory, with separate short-term and long-term stores. Example: Kabir is asked to buy 15 items at the market without a list; he remembers the first few (milk, eggs, bread) and the last few, but forgets the middle ones. Option c is right because it is the only option that names both peaks, primacy and recency. Exam trap: options that pair the beginning with the middle, or the middle with the end, describe a curve that does not exist; the middle is always the trough.", "traps": {"a": "This keeps primacy but puts the middle of the list high; the middle is actually the lowest point of the U-shaped curve, where items get neither extra rehearsal nor the benefit of being recent.", "b": "This describes primacy with a steady decline and leaves out the recency effect; recall rises again for the last items because they are still in short-term memory.", "d": "This keeps recency but wrongly raises the middle and drops primacy; the first items are recalled well because they are rehearsed most and reach long-term memory."}}, {"q": "The degree to which construct system allows new information to enter that will allow construct system to change is called", "concept": "The answer is b: in George Kelly's (1955, The Psychology of Personal Constructs) theory, permeability is the degree to which a construct admits new elements, so that the construct system can take in new experience and change. Kelly pictured each person as a scientist who builds personal constructs, bipolar dimensions such as friendly versus hostile or safe versus risky, to predict events. His theory has one fundamental postulate (a person's processes are channelled by the ways he or she anticipates events) and eleven corollaries. The modulation corollary says that change in a construct system is limited by the permeability of the constructs within whose range the changes lie. A permeable construct can take in events it has never been applied to before; an impermeable one is closed, so new evidence bounces off it, as in rigid prejudice. Kelly measured constructs with the Role Construct Repertory Test (Rep test), which asks how two people are alike and different from a third. Example: Riya once construed classmates only as \"toppers versus failures\". After joining a theatre group she lets that construct take in new people and new qualities, such as creative and kind, and her whole way of judging people shifts; her construct was permeable. Option b is right because permeability is exactly the property that decides whether new information can enter and revise the construct system. Exam trap: constructive alternativism also sounds like openness to change, but it is Kelly's broad philosophy that any event can be construed in alternative ways, not the property of a particular construct.", "traps": {"a": "The individuality corollary (printed here as 'Individual Corollary') says people differ from one another in how they construe events; it is about differences between people, not about a system's openness to new information.", "c": "Loose construing means a construct gives varying, shifting predictions (the opposite of tight construing); Kelly saw it as one phase of the creativity cycle, not as the capacity to admit new elements.", "d": "Constructive alternativism is Kelly's basic philosophical assumption that any event can be reinterpreted in many ways; it is the stance of the whole theory, not a property of individual constructs."}}, {"q": "Who among the following has developed a test for personality assessment where the subject has to choose between a pair of jokes?", "concept": "The answer is d: the personality test in which the subject chooses the funnier of a pair of jokes comes from Raymond B. Cattell, the British-American trait theorist. Raymond B. Cattell (1905 to 1998) used factor analysis to find 16 source traits and built the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF, 1949). He also believed personality shows in what a person finds funny. Raymond Cattell and Lester Luborsky (1947), in \"Personality factors in response to humor\", factor analysed people's preferences among large sets of jokes and found that humour choices cluster into a few broad factors linked to personality. This line of work became the IPAT Humor Test of Personality, published by Cattell's Institute for Personality and Ability Testing. Each item presents two short jokes and the person marks the one that seems funnier, a forced choice format, and the pattern of choices is scored on humour dimensions that reflect personality. Because the person does not know what is being measured, the test is a disguised, indirect way to assess personality. Example: Arjun keeps choosing jokes built on clever wordplay over jokes about embarrassment; his friend Kabir does the reverse, and on the humour test their choices would load on different factors. Option d is right because R.B. Cattell is the Cattell who studied humour and personality through factor analysis. Exam trap: two famous Cattells appear in exams. James McKeen Cattell (1860 to 1944) coined the term \"mental test\" (1890) and measured reaction time and sensory abilities; he did not build personality tests.", "traps": {"a": "No test author called McCafe appears in standard psychology or testing texts; the name is a distractor.", "b": "William McCall was an educational measurement specialist who introduced the T-score (How to Measure in Education, 1922); he did not build a humour-based personality test.", "c": "James McKeen Cattell coined 'mental test' (1890) and measured reaction time and sensory acuity as signs of intelligence; the humour test belongs to the other Cattell, Raymond."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Working memory can hold a large amount of information even though it can retain only seven to nine items. Reason (R): Items in working memory contain several separate bits of information grouped together called chunks.", "concept": "The answer is a: both statements are true, and chunking (R) is exactly why a store limited to about seven items can still hold a large amount of information (A). George Miller (1956), in \"The magical number seven, plus or minus two\", showed that immediate memory holds about 7 units, give or take 2, so 5 to 9. Judge A: the limit is set in units, not in raw information. Seven words carry far more information than seven single digits, yet both fill the span, so a store of seven to nine items can hold a lot. A is true. Judge R: a chunk is a meaningful unit formed by grouping separate bits with the help of knowledge already in long-term memory, such as a word, a date or an acronym. William Chase and Herbert Simon (1973) found that chess masters recall whole board positions at a glance because they see familiar patterns of pieces as single chunks. R is true. Does R explain A? Yes: capacity is counted in chunks, and each chunk packs several bits, so the total information held can be large even though the number of items is small. Example: Meera cannot repeat the 12 letters B J P I S R O R B I U N heard once, but regrouped as BJP, ISRO, RBI and UN they become four easy chunks. Option a is right because R gives the very mechanism that makes A possible. Exam trap: \"seven items\" and \"large amount of information\" seem to clash, so students mark A false; the clash disappears once items are understood as chunks. Note too that Miller wrote of short-term memory; the working memory model came later (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974), and Nelson Cowan (2001) puts the limit nearer 4 chunks.", "traps": {"b": "This would mean R is true but irrelevant; in fact chunking is the very reason a span of about seven items can carry a lot of information, so R does explain A.", "c": "This calls R false, but chunking (George Miller, 1956) is a well established finding, seen in chess masters and in anyone who reads a phone number in groups.", "d": "This calls A false, but because the span is counted in meaningful units rather than in bits, a store of seven to nine items can indeed hold a large amount of information."}}, {"q": "Who has emphasized the linguistic relativity hypothesis?", "concept": "The answer is d: the linguistic relativity hypothesis is tied to Benjamin Lee Whorf (1956, Language, Thought, and Reality, published after his death), who built on the ideas of his teacher Edward Sapir (1929), so it is also called the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis. It claims that the language you speak shapes how you think about and perceive the world. It comes in two strengths. The strong form, linguistic determinism, says language fixes thought, so speakers of very different languages live in different mental worlds. The weak form, linguistic relativity proper, says language only nudges what we notice, remember and find easy to think about. Whorf argued, for example, that the Hopi language treats time differently from English, so Hopi speakers think about time differently. Later research challenged the strong form. Eleanor Rosch (1972, 1973) studied the Dani of New Guinea, whose language has only two basic colour terms, and found they still learned and remembered the focal colours more easily than other shades, much as English speakers do. Weaker effects of language on memory and attention do find support. Example: Kabir speaks a language with separate words for a mother's brother (mama) and a father's brother (chacha), so he may notice that family distinction more readily than an English speaker who says only \"uncle\". Option d is right because Whorf is the name most closely tied to the claim that language shapes thought. Exam trap: Noam Chomsky and Steven Pinker are famous language theorists, but both argue for a universal, inborn capacity for language that does not lock thought into a particular language, the reverse of Whorf.", "traps": {"a": "Noam Chomsky (1957, Syntactic Structures; the language acquisition device) stressed an inborn universal grammar shared by all humans, not the idea that each language moulds thought differently.", "b": "Jean Piaget held that thought develops first and that language reflects the child's cognitive stage, the reverse direction to linguistic relativity.", "c": "Steven Pinker (1994, The Language Instinct) treats language as an evolved instinct and is a well known critic of the Whorf hypothesis, arguing that people think in a language-independent 'mentalese'."}}, {"q": "Telegraphic speech is typical of the ______ stage.", "concept": "The answer is c: telegraphic speech is typical of the two-word stage, which most children reach at about 18 to 24 months. The name compares toddler speech to an old telegram, where senders paid by the word and kept only the essential ones. Roger Brown (1973, A First Language) described how children at this stage keep content words (nouns, verbs, adjectives) and drop function words such as articles, prepositions and auxiliary verbs, along with endings such as the plural -s or the past tense -ed. So a child says \"want milk\", \"daddy go\" or \"more juice\", yet the word order already follows the adult language, a sign of early grammar. The usual sequence is: cooing (vowel-like sounds, about 2 months), babbling (consonant-vowel strings such as \"bababa\", about 6 months), the one-word or holophrastic stage (about 12 months, a single word doing the work of a sentence), the two-word telegraphic stage (about 18 to 24 months), and then multiword speech with grammatical endings, when overregularisation errors such as \"two foots\" appear. Example: two-year-old Arjun points at the window and says \"car go\"; he means \"the car is going away\" and leaves out every small word. Option c is right because telegraphic speech is defined by these two-word combinations of content words. Exam trap: the holophrase of the one-word stage also carries a whole sentence's meaning, so students confuse the two; a holophrase is one word, while telegraphic speech strings at least two words together. Key note: telegraphic features carry on into early three-word utterances, but textbooks classically tie the term to the two-word stage; it is the official UGC answer after grievances, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Babbling (about 6 months) is repeated consonant-vowel syllables such as 'bababa' with no real words yet, so there is nothing telegraphic about it.", "b": "The one-word stage (about 12 months) uses holophrases, single words standing for whole ideas; telegraphic speech begins only when words are combined.", "d": "Three-word utterances can still sound telegraphic, but they come after the two-word stage, which is the stage textbooks name for telegraphic speech."}}, {"q": "The sentence \"Blue Jeans wear false smiles\" has correct _____ but incorrect _______.", "concept": "The answer is d: \"Blue jeans wear false smiles\" has correct syntax but incorrect semantics. Language is analysed at several levels. Phonemes are the smallest units of sound that change meaning (the /b/ and /p/ that separate \"bat\" from \"pat\"). Morphemes are the smallest units of meaning (\"jean\", the plural \"-s\", \"smile\"). Syntax is the set of rules for combining words into grammatical sentences. Semantics is the study of meaning, of words and of whole sentences. Check the sentence: an adjective and noun as subject (blue jeans), a verb (wear), and an adjective and noun as object (false smiles). That is perfect English word order, so the syntax is correct. But jeans are not living things and cannot wear smiles, so the sentence makes no literal sense; the semantics fails. Noam Chomsky (1957, Syntactic Structures) made the same point with \"Colourless green ideas sleep furiously\": a sentence can be grammatical yet meaningless, which shows that syntax is separate from meaning. Every phoneme and morpheme in the jeans sentence is a real English unit used properly, so those levels are fine. Example: if Riya's little brother says \"Milk the drinks cat\", each word means something but the order breaks the rules, an error of syntax; \"The cat drinks milk\" is correct at both levels. Option d is right because the grammatical form is correct while the meaning is not. Exam trap: option c names the same two terms in reverse order; read carefully which blank is \"correct\" and which is \"incorrect\".", "traps": {"a": "Morphemes are indeed correct here, but so are the phonemes, since every sound is a normal English sound; the fault lies in meaning, not in sound units.", "b": "Phonemes are correct and so are the morphemes (jean, -s, smile, -s); the fault is not in word parts but in the meaning of the whole sentence.", "c": "This reverses the answer: the word order is grammatical (syntax correct) while the meaning is impossible (semantics incorrect), not the other way round."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): In deduction, conclusion is derived from a premise. Reason (R): People reason by using deduction as well as induction.", "concept": "The answer is b: both statements are true, but R does not explain A. Judge A on its own: deductive reasoning draws a conclusion from one or more premises, and if the premises are true and the argument is valid, the conclusion must be true. The classic case is the syllogism, first set out by Aristotle: all humans are mortal; Socrates is human; so Socrates is mortal. Deduction runs from the general to the particular. A is true. Judge R on its own: inductive reasoning goes the other way, from particular observations to a probable general rule (\"every swan I have seen is white, so all swans are white\"), and its conclusions can be overturned by new evidence. People use both every day. Peter Wason (1966) showed with his four-card selection task that people often fail at formal deduction, and Philip Johnson-Laird (1983, Mental Models) argued that people reason by building mental models of the premises. R is true. Now ask whether R explains A. A states what deduction is. R only reports that people use deduction alongside induction. That fact does not tell us why a deductive conclusion follows from its premises; that comes from the logical form of the argument. So R is true but beside the point. Example: Kabir reasons \"all trains to Pune leave from platform 4; this is the Pune train; so it leaves from platform 4\" (deduction), and also \"the last five Monday trains were late, so Monday trains are usually late\" (induction). Option b is right because both statements hold, but R does not give the reason for A. Exam trap: R mentions deduction, so it looks connected; a shared word is not an explanation.", "traps": {"a": "This needs R to explain A, but the fact that people use both deduction and induction says nothing about why a deductive conclusion follows from its premises.", "c": "This calls R false, yet people clearly use both kinds of reasoning, as studies of syllogisms, Peter Wason's selection task and everyday generalisations show.", "d": "This calls A false, but deriving a conclusion from premises is the very definition of deduction, as in the syllogism."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Phenomenon) with List-II (Psychological name): p. Smallest unit that carries meaning, q. Rules to communicate and understand others, r. Rules for deriving meanings, s. Rules for combining words. 1. Grammar, 2. Semantics, 3. Morphemes, 4. Syntax", "concept": "The answer is c: the smallest unit that carries meaning goes with morphemes (p-3), the rules to communicate with and understand others go with grammar (q-1), the rules for deriving meanings go with semantics (r-2), and the rules for combining words go with syntax (s-4). Language is studied at levels that build on one another. At the bottom are phonemes, the smallest sound units that change meaning (English has about 40). Phonemes combine into morphemes, the smallest units that carry meaning: \"unhappiness\" has three, \"un\", \"happy\" and \"ness\", and the plural \"-s\" is a morpheme too. Grammar is the whole system of rules in a language that lets speakers produce messages and understand others. Within it, semantics is the set of rules for deriving meaning from morphemes, words and sentences, so we know that \"-ed\" signals the past and that \"bank\" means one thing in \"river bank\" and another in \"bank loan\". Syntax is the set of rules for ordering words into sentences, such as subject, verb, object in English. Pragmatics, the social use of language in context, is a further level not listed here. Example: when Meera hears \"The baby is unhappy\", syntax tells her the word order is legal, semantics lets her combine \"un\" and \"happy\" into \"not happy\", and her grammar as a whole lets her understand and reply. Option c is right because it is the only code that gives each of the four phenomena its correct term. Exam trap: semantics and syntax are both \"rules\", so codes swap them; semantics is about meaning, syntax about word order. Every option fixes p-3, so the decision rests on q, r and s.", "traps": {"a": "This keeps morphemes and grammar right but swaps semantics and syntax, giving the rules for deriving meaning to syntax and the rules for combining words to semantics.", "b": "This pairs the communication rules with semantics, the meaning rules with syntax and the word-combining rules with grammar; only the morpheme pairing is right.", "d": "This correctly gives combining words to syntax but swaps grammar and semantics, calling the overall communication rules semantics and the meaning rules grammar."}}, {"q": "Three types of feature detector cells are", "concept": "The answer is d: the three types of feature detector cells are simple cells, complex cells and hypercomplex cells. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959, 1962) recorded the electrical activity of single neurons in the visual cortex of anaesthetised cats, and later monkeys, while projecting lines and bars onto a screen. They found cells tuned to specific features of the image, so these came to be called feature detectors. Simple cells, in the primary visual (striate) cortex, fire most to a bar or edge of one particular orientation at one particular place in the receptive field; their fields have separate excitatory and inhibitory zones. Complex cells also prefer a particular orientation but respond wherever the bar falls within a larger field, and many prefer movement in one direction. Hypercomplex cells, described by Hubel and Wiesel in 1965 and now usually called end-stopped cells, respond best to a bar of a particular length, or to corners and angles, and fire less when the bar runs past the end of the field. This hierarchy suggested that vision builds complex shapes out of simple features. Hubel and Wiesel shared the 1981 Nobel Prize with Roger Sperry. Example: as Riya reads the letter \"L\", simple cells respond to its vertical and horizontal strokes, complex cells keep responding as her eyes shift slightly, and end-stopped cells signal the corner. Option d is right because it lists the three classic cell types and nothing else. Exam trap: the wrong options mix in real cells from outside this system; glial cells and T-cells exist, but they are not feature detectors, so any list containing them fails.", "traps": {"a": "Simple cells are feature detectors, but glial cells are support cells (myelin, nutrients, clean-up) and T-cells are immune cells; neither responds to visual features.", "b": "Simple and complex cells are correct, but glial cells do not detect features; the missing third type is the hypercomplex (end-stopped) cell.", "c": "Hypercomplex cells are feature detectors, but 'sensory cells' is a general term for receptors and T-cells belong to the immune system, so the list mixes unrelated cells."}}, {"q": "Assertion (A): Sleep fulfills a critical biological function. Reason (R): Sleep is required to restore the homeostasis.", "concept": "The answer is a: both statements are correct, and R is taken as the explanation of A. Judge A: sleep serves a vital biological function. People kept awake show sleepiness, low mood, lapses of attention, microsleeps of a few seconds, and weaker immune and metabolic function. Allan Rechtschaffen and colleagues (1983) kept rats awake on a rotating disc, and total sleep deprivation killed them within a few weeks. So A is true. Judge R: restorative (recuperation) theories, argued by Ian Oswald (1980) among others, say sleep repairs and rebalances what waking runs down. Alexander Borbely's (1982) two-process model describes a homeostatic sleep pressure (Process S) that builds the longer we stay awake and is discharged during sleep, especially slow-wave sleep, alongside a circadian Process C. Lulu Xie and colleagues (2013) found that the brain clears waste products faster during sleep through the glymphatic system. R is true. Does R explain A? On this reading, yes: sleep is biologically critical because it restores the internal balance (homeostasis) that waking disturbs. Example: after Arjun stays up all night before an exam, his next night has extra deep sleep; the built-up sleep pressure is paid back, a homeostatic pattern. Option a is right because R gives a reason, homeostatic restoration, for why sleep is biologically critical. Exam trap: students often treat \"sleep is necessary\" and \"sleep restores homeostasis\" as two unrelated facts and pick b. Key note: the function of sleep is still debated; the adaptive or evolutionary view (Wilse Webb, 1974; Ray Meddis, 1975) and memory consolidation are rivals to restoration, so some would pick b. The official UGC answer after grievances is a, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "This accepts both statements but denies the link; on the restorative view the homeostatic rebalancing done in sleep is exactly why sleep is biologically critical, so R is taken as the explanation.", "c": "This calls A false, but sleep deprivation studies in humans and rats show that sleep is biologically essential.", "d": "This calls R false, but homeostatic sleep pressure (Alexander Borbely's Process S) and recovery sleep after deprivation show that sleep does restore homeostasis."}}, {"q": "Choose the odd one for pattern of sympathetic activation during emotions:", "concept": "The answer is b: pupil constriction is the odd one out, because it is a parasympathetic response, while the other three are signs of sympathetic activation. The autonomic nervous system has two branches that usually work in opposition. The sympathetic branch prepares the body for action, the fight or flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915). During strong emotions such as fear or anger it raises heart rate and blood pressure, speeds breathing and widens the airways, dilates the pupils (mydriasis) to let in more light, releases glucose for energy, slows digestion and increases sweating. Sweat is salty water that conducts electricity, so sweating lowers skin resistance (raises skin conductance); this galvanic skin response is one of the channels a polygraph records. The parasympathetic branch is the rest and digest system: it slows the heart, stimulates digestion and constricts the pupils (miosis) through fibres in the oculomotor nerve. Example: when a dog suddenly lunges at Meera on the street, her heart pounds, she breathes fast, her palms sweat and her pupils widen; once she is safe at home, parasympathetic activity slows her heart and her pupils return to normal. Option b is right because constriction of the pupil is the parasympathetic opposite of the sympathetic dilation seen in emotion. Exam trap: \"lowered skin resistance\" sounds like a decrease and so like calming, but it reflects more sweating and is a classic sign of sympathetic arousal.", "traps": {"a": "Increased heart rate is a core sympathetic effect that pumps more blood to the muscles for fight or flight, so it belongs in the pattern.", "c": "Lowered skin resistance comes from sympathetic activation of the sweat glands; it is the galvanic skin response used in lie detection, so it belongs.", "d": "Faster breathing, with widened airways, brings in more oxygen during sympathetic arousal, so it belongs in the pattern."}}, {"q": "Out of following who has not divided intelligence into two general components?", "concept": "The answer is d: Louis Thurstone did not divide intelligence into two general components; he proposed several primary abilities. Louis Thurstone (1938, Primary Mental Abilities) gave a large battery of tests to students and analysed them with his method of multiple factor analysis. Instead of one general factor he found about seven primary mental abilities: verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, memory, perceptual speed and reasoning. He later accepted that these abilities correlate, but his model is a multiple-factor one, not a two-part split. Each of the other names proposed a two-way division. Raymond Cattell (1963), later with John Horn (1966), separated fluid intelligence (reasoning with new problems, declining with age) from crystallised intelligence (accumulated knowledge, holding up with age). Arthur Jensen (1969) separated Level I associative ability (rote memory, digit span) from Level II conceptual ability (abstract reasoning, close to g). Donald Hebb (1949, The Organization of Behavior) separated Intelligence A, the brain's innate potential, from Intelligence B, the level of functioning that develops through experience. Example: Riya's skill with a new puzzle and her vocabulary from years of reading are Cattell's two components; her separate scores on number, space and verbal tests are Thurstone's style. Option d is right because Thurstone's model has around seven abilities, not two. Exam trap: Thurstone and Cattell are both factor theorists, so students lump them together; count the components. Key note: the paper prints \"Cattell J.M.\", but the fluid and crystallised split is Raymond B. Cattell's; James McKeen Cattell made no such split, so read option a as R.B. Cattell. Thurstone is the clear answer either way; it is the official UGC answer after grievances, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Read as Raymond B. Cattell (the printed J.M. is a slip), this option did split intelligence in two, into fluid and crystallised intelligence, so it does not answer the question.", "b": "Arthur Jensen (1969) divided mental ability into Level I associative learning and Level II conceptual reasoning, a clear two-component split.", "c": "Donald Hebb (1949) separated Intelligence A (innate brain potential) from Intelligence B (functioning shaped by experience), a two-component split."}}, {"q": "Number of different factors as specified by Spearman to explain the performance on some tests:", "concept": "The answer is d: Charles Spearman explained test performance with one general factor, g, and many specific factors, s. Charles Spearman (1904), in \"General intelligence, objectively determined and measured\", noticed that children's scores on very different school subjects and sensory tasks all correlated positively, a pattern now called the positive manifold. To explain it he developed an early form of factor analysis and proposed the two-factor theory, set out fully in The Abilities of Man (1927). Every intellectual task draws on a single general factor, g, which he likened to mental energy, and also on a specific factor, s, unique to that task. Because there is only one g, it explains why all the tests correlate; because each test has its own s, it explains why the correlations are not perfect. The number of s factors therefore equals the number of different tests, so there are many. Reasoning tests built on abstract relations, such as Raven's Progressive Matrices (John Raven, 1938), are among the purest measures of g. Later models added group factors between g and s, as in Philip Vernon's (1950) hierarchical model. Example: Kabir does well in arithmetic, vocabulary and puzzles because of a high g, but his outstanding spelling reflects an extra specific ability, his s for spelling. Option d is right because Spearman's theory has exactly one g and as many s factors as there are tests. Exam trap: the letters get reversed; g is general and single, s is specific and many. Do not confuse Spearman's two kinds of factor with Raymond Cattell's (1963) two kinds of general ability (fluid and crystallised).", "traps": {"a": "This reverses Spearman: there is only one g (general) and many s (specific) factors, not one s and many g.", "b": "Spearman insisted on a single general factor running through all tests; many g factors would destroy the very idea of general intelligence.", "c": "One g is correct, but there is not just one s; each test has its own specific factor, so there are as many s factors as there are tests."}}, {"q": "Which set of three approaches is more relevant to study the impact of Genetic factors on intelligence? 1. Correlation between siblings, 2. Correlation between identical twins reared apart, 3. Adopted child studies, 4. Correlation between parents and children", "concept": "The answer is c: correlations between siblings, between identical twins reared apart, and between parents and children (1, 2 and 4) form the set taken as most relevant here. Behaviour genetics tests heredity by asking whether people who share more genes are more alike in IQ, the family resemblance or kinship correlation method. Siblings share on average half their varying genes and correlate about 0.45 to 0.5 in IQ. Parents and their children also share half their genes and correlate about 0.4. Identical (monozygotic) twins share all their genes, and if they grow up in different homes their likeness cannot come from a shared upbringing, which makes them a strong test of heredity. Thomas Bouchard and Matt McGue (1981) reviewed 111 studies and found that identical twins reared apart correlate about 0.7, far above unrelated people, and the Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart (Thomas Bouchard and colleagues, 1990) found a similar figure. Resemblance that rises with genetic relatedness is the core evidence that genes influence intelligence. Example: if Arjun and his identical twin, separated at birth and raised in Delhi and Chennai, still score within a few points of each other, that likeness points to their shared genes. Option c is right because all three approaches measure IQ resemblance against degree of genetic relatedness. Exam trap: adoption studies are also a genuine behaviour genetics method, which is why options containing 3 tempt students. Key note: adoption studies, which compare adopted children with their biological and adoptive parents (as in the Texas and Colorado Adoption Projects), are an equally standard method, so this item is debatable. The official UGC answer after grievances is c, which groups the three correlation-by-kinship approaches, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "This keeps siblings and twins reared apart but swaps parent-child correlations for adoption studies; the intended set keeps the three kinship correlations together.", "b": "This keeps twins reared apart and parent-child correlations but replaces sibling correlations with adoption studies, breaking the kinship-correlation set.", "d": "This drops identical twins reared apart, the single strongest test of heredity in the list, so it cannot be the most relevant set."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Psychologist) with List-II (Concept): p. Adler, q. Bandura, r. Jung, s. Horney. 1. Basic anxiety, 2. Triadic Reciprocal causation, 3. Fictional Finalism, 4. Principle of Equivalence", "concept": "The answer is c: Adler goes with fictional finalism (p-3), Bandura with triadic reciprocal causation (q-2), Jung with the principle of equivalence (r-4) and Horney with basic anxiety (s-1). Alfred Adler (1912, The Neurotic Constitution) borrowed from Hans Vaihinger's The Philosophy of 'As If' (1911) the idea that people live by useful fictions. Fictional finalism is an imagined future goal, such as \"I must be respected\", that is never fully real yet pulls behaviour forward. Albert Bandura (1978) called his view reciprocal determinism and later, in Social Foundations of Thought and Action (1986), triadic reciprocal causation: personal factors (beliefs, expectations), behaviour and environment all influence one another in both directions. Carl Jung (1928, On Psychic Energy) treated libido as general psychic energy obeying two principles taken from physics. The principle of equivalence says energy withdrawn from one activity or value does not vanish but reappears in another; the principle of entropy says energy flows from stronger to weaker values, towards balance. Karen Horney (1937, The Neurotic Personality of Our Time) traced neurosis to basic anxiety, the child's feeling of being isolated and helpless in a potentially hostile world, which drives moving toward, against or away from people. Example: when Meera gives up competitive dance and pours the same passion into teaching children, Jung would say the principle of equivalence is at work. Option c is right because it is the only code in which all four pairings are correct. Exam trap: option d keeps Adler and Horney right but trades Bandura's and Jung's concepts; remember that Bandura's three-way causation joins person, behaviour and environment, while equivalence is about energy moving between interests.", "traps": {"a": "This gives Adler triadic reciprocal causation (Bandura's), Bandura the principle of equivalence (Jung's) and Jung fictional finalism (Adler's); only Horney with basic anxiety is right.", "b": "This gives Adler basic anxiety (Horney's), Bandura fictional finalism (Adler's), Jung triadic reciprocal causation (Bandura's) and Horney the principle of equivalence (Jung's), so every pairing is wrong.", "d": "Adler with fictional finalism and Horney with basic anxiety are right, but it gives Bandura the principle of equivalence and Jung triadic reciprocal causation, swapping their concepts."}}, {"q": "Researchers have found different ways in which an individual's genotype shapes his or her environment. Which of the following is NOT one of them?", "concept": "The answer is d: differential sensitivity of different genotypes to the same environment is not a way in which the genotype shapes the environment; it is gene-environment interaction. The stem is about gene-environment correlation, the finding that genes and environments are not independent because genes partly select or create the environments people meet. Robert Plomin, John DeFries and John Loehlin (1977) named three kinds, and Sandra Scarr and Kathleen McCartney (1983) turned them into a developmental theory. Passive correlation: parents pass on both genes and a home that suits those genes, as when musical parents give a child musical genes and a house full of instruments; it is strongest in infancy. Evocative (reactive) correlation: the child's inherited traits draw particular responses, as a cheerful, smiling baby is picked up and played with more. Active correlation, or niche picking: people seek out settings that suit their genetic leanings, as a sociable teenager joins the drama club; it grows with age as children gain choice. Option d describes something different: two people in the same environment react differently because of their genes. Avshalom Caspi and colleagues (2002) found that maltreated boys with a low-activity MAOA gene variant were more likely to become antisocial than maltreated boys with the high-activity variant. That is the genotype changing the response to the environment, not shaping the environment itself. Example: energetic Kabir keeps asking for football coaching (active), and his coach gives him extra attention because of his enthusiasm (evocative). Option d is right because it is the only statement about sensitivity to the environment rather than about the genotype creating the environment. Exam trap: interaction and correlation sound alike; correlation is about which environments you get, interaction about how you respond to them.", "traps": {"a": "Passive gene-environment correlation is one of the three recognised kinds: parents provide both the genes and a home environment that matches them, so it does belong.", "b": "Evocative (reactive) gene-environment correlation is a recognised kind: a child's inherited traits draw particular reactions from people and surroundings, so it belongs.", "c": "Active gene-environment correlation, or niche picking, is a recognised kind: the child seeks out environments that fit his or her genotype, so it belongs."}}, {"q": "Match List-I (Types of Variable) with List-II (Example): p. Continuous, q. Real discrete, r. Artificially discrete, s. Qualitative. 1. Selection test outcome in terms of selected/rejected, 2. Voice pitch, 3. Intelligence, 4. Gender", "concept": "The answer is d: intelligence goes with continuous (p-3), gender with real discrete (q-4), the selection outcome of selected or rejected with artificially discrete (r-1), and voice pitch with qualitative (s-2). A continuous variable can take any value along an unbroken scale, so between any two values there is always another; intelligence is treated as a continuous trait even though tests report whole-number scores. A discrete variable comes in separate categories with nothing in between. In a real (true) discrete variable the categories exist in nature, such as gender as classically taught, or alive versus dead. In an artificially discrete variable, a continuum is cut into categories by the researcher: candidates' scores run smoothly, but a cut-off divides them into selected and rejected. This matters for statistics: a true dichotomy correlated with a continuous variable calls for the point-biserial correlation, an artificial dichotomy for the biserial correlation. A qualitative variable is described by kind rather than amount; on this list voice pitch is the item left to be judged by its quality (high, low, husky, shrill). Example: in Riya's recruitment study, aptitude scores are continuous, gender is real discrete, the hire-or-reject decision is artificially discrete, and the interviewer's note of a \"soft, husky voice\" is qualitative. Option d is right because it is the only code that gets the three clear pairings right and leaves voice pitch as the qualitative item. Exam trap: a pass or fail result looks like a natural split, but it is artificial, because the cut-off is placed on a continuous score. Key note: voice pitch is physically continuous (frequency in hertz), so filing it as qualitative only works if pitch is judged as a quality; but d is the only code with the three clear pairings, and it is the official UGC answer after grievances. The answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "This makes gender continuous, the selection outcome real discrete, voice pitch artificially discrete and intelligence qualitative; none of these fit, and intelligence is the clearest continuous example.", "b": "Gender as real discrete and the selection outcome as artificially discrete are right, and pitch is physically continuous, but this code then has to call intelligence qualitative, which fails because intelligence is measured as an amount.", "c": "Intelligence as continuous is right, but a selection outcome is not real discrete (its cut-off is man-made) and gender is not artificially discrete (its categories exist in nature)."}}, {"q": "From the passage (Classical Test Theory: every obtained score is the true score plus error of measurement; split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach alpha, Spearman-Brown, test-retest and interscorer reliability; content, concurrent, predictive and construct validity; factor analysis). Reliability is conceptualized as", "concept": "The answer is b: in Classical Test Theory, reliability is the proportion of obtained variance that is true variance, that is, true variance divided by (true variance plus error variance). Classical Test Theory, founded by Charles Spearman (1904) and set out in full by Harold Gulliksen (1950, Theory of Mental Tests), starts from one equation: every obtained score X equals a true score T plus a random error E, so X = T + E. Errors are assumed to be random, to average zero over many testings, and to be uncorrelated with true scores. Because T and E are uncorrelated, their variances simply add: obtained variance = true variance + error variance. Reliability is then true variance / obtained variance, which is the same as true variance / (true variance + error variance). It runs from 0 (all error) to 1 (no error). A reliability of 0.80 means 80 percent of the variance in people's scores reflects true differences and 20 percent reflects error. The square root of reliability, the index of reliability, is the correlation between obtained and true scores, the consistency between true and obtained scores that the passage describes. The standard error of measurement follows from it: the standard deviation multiplied by the square root of (1 minus r). Example: on Arjun's anxiety scale, if true variance is 40 and error variance is 10, reliability is 40 / 50 = 0.80. Option b is right because it puts true variance on top and the full obtained variance (true plus error) underneath. Exam trap: option a turns the ratio upside down, which would give values of 1 or more; a reliability coefficient can never exceed 1.", "traps": {"a": "This flips the ratio: obtained variance is always at least as large as true variance, so obtained over true would be 1 or more, which no reliability coefficient can be.", "c": "True variance divided by error variance is a signal-to-noise ratio that can run far above 1; reliability divides true variance by the total obtained variance.", "d": "Obtained variance over error variance is the reciprocal of the proportion of error; it has no place in the Classical Test Theory definition of reliability."}}, {"q": "From the passage (Classical Test Theory: every obtained score is the true score plus error of measurement; split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach alpha, Spearman-Brown, test-retest and interscorer reliability; content, concurrent, predictive and construct validity; factor analysis). Cronbach alpha is: 1. larger than split half reliability coefficient, 2. smaller than reliability coefficient, 3. average of all possible split-half reliability coefficients, 4. equal to Kuder-Richardson reliability for dichotomous items", "concept": "The answer is c: statements 3 and 4 are correct; Cronbach's alpha is the average of all possible split-half reliability coefficients, and for items scored right or wrong it equals the Kuder-Richardson reliability (KR-20). Lee Cronbach (1951), in \"Coefficient alpha and the internal structure of tests\", generalised earlier internal consistency formulas. A split-half reliability divides a test into two halves (for example odd versus even items) and correlates them, but the result depends on which split you choose. Cronbach showed that alpha equals the mean of the coefficients from every possible split (each computed by the Rulon or Flanagan formula), which removes that arbitrariness. Alpha is computed from item variances: alpha = [k / (k minus 1)] multiplied by [1 minus (sum of item variances / total test variance)]. When items are scored 0 or 1, each item variance is p times q, and the formula becomes formula 20 of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937). So alpha works for any item format, including Likert ratings, while KR-20 is its special case for dichotomous items. Statement 1 is false: alpha is an average, so some split-halves are larger than it and some smaller. Example: on Meera's 20-item true-false quiz, KR-20 and alpha give the same value, 0.78. Option c is right because only 3 and 4 are the standard textbook properties of alpha. Exam trap: students remember that alpha is \"conservative\" and pick 2, or think an average must exceed its parts and pick 1. Key note: alpha is a lower bound to true reliability, so some read statement 2 as partly true, and the split-half equality holds exactly only for Rulon-type split-halves; but 3 and 4 are the textbook properties and no option pairs 2 with them. It is the official UGC answer after grievances, and the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Statement 1 is false: alpha is the mean of all split-half coefficients, so it cannot be larger than every split-half; some splits give higher values than alpha.", "b": "Alpha is a lower bound to true reliability but can equal it when items are equivalent, so 'smaller than' is not its defining property; choosing 2 alone also leaves out the two textbook properties, 3 and 4.", "d": "Statement 4 is true (alpha equals KR-20 for right-wrong items), but statement 1 is false, since alpha is the average of the split-halves, not larger than them."}}, {"q": "From the passage (Classical Test Theory: every obtained score is the true score plus error of measurement; split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach alpha, Spearman-Brown, test-retest and interscorer reliability; content, concurrent, predictive and construct validity; factor analysis). The interscorer reliability is especially important for: 1. Projective tests, 2. Achievement tests with short answers, 3. Multiple choice tests, 4. Judgements by multiple raters", "concept": "The answer is d: interscorer reliability matters most for projective tests, short-answer achievement tests and judgements by multiple raters (1, 2 and 4), but not for multiple-choice tests. Interscorer (inter-rater) reliability is the degree to which two or more people who score the same responses independently arrive at the same results. It is estimated by correlating their scores, by percentage agreement, by Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960), which corrects agreement for chance, or by the intraclass correlation. It becomes a concern whenever scoring needs human judgement. In projective tests, scoring involves interpretation: two psychologists may code the same Rorschach response differently, or read different themes into a Thematic Apperception Test story, which is why the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) trained scorers and reported their agreement. In short-answer and essay achievement tests, examiners must decide how much credit a partly correct answer deserves, so marks can vary across scorers. When several raters judge behaviour, as in classroom observation, interviews or performance appraisal, their agreement must be checked before the ratings can be trusted. A multiple-choice test, by contrast, is scored against a fixed answer sheet, by hand or by machine, so any two scorers get the same total and scorer error is close to zero. Example: two teachers mark Kabir's short-answer history paper and give him 14 and 18 out of 20, a sign of poor interscorer reliability; on his multiple-choice science test both get 17. Option d is right because it lists exactly the three situations in which judgement enters scoring. Exam trap: multiple-choice tests are also \"tests\", so students tick 3 too; objective scoring removes judgement, so interscorer reliability is not an issue there.", "traps": {"a": "Projective tests do need interscorer reliability, but so do short-answer tests and multiple-rater judgements, so 1 alone is incomplete.", "b": "Short-answer tests (2) belong, but multiple-choice tests (3) are scored against a fixed set of correct answers, so scorer differences hardly arise; the option also leaves out projective tests and multiple raters.", "c": "Projective tests and multiple raters belong, but short-answer achievement tests also involve scorer judgement about partial credit, so they must be included."}}, {"q": "From the passage (Classical Test Theory: every obtained score is the true score plus error of measurement; split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach alpha, Spearman-Brown, test-retest and interscorer reliability; content, concurrent, predictive and construct validity; factor analysis). Consider that a newly developed test for assessing abstract reasoning and a well established test of abstract reasoning are administered to a sample of 350 and a correlation of 0.25, significant at .01 level is obtained. It will be inferred that the new test has", "concept": "The answer is a: the new test has unsatisfactory concurrent validity. Concurrent validity is a kind of criterion-related validity in which a test is correlated with a criterion measured at about the same time, here an established test of abstract reasoning. Because both tests are given to the same sample together, this is concurrent, not predictive, validity; predictive validity would need a criterion measured later, such as exam results a year on. The real question is whether r = 0.25 is good enough. It is significant at the .01 level mainly because the sample is large (N = 350); with that many people even a small correlation is unlikely to be zero by chance. But significance says only that some relationship exists, not that it is strong. Squaring the correlation gives the coefficient of determination: 0.25 multiplied by 0.25 is 0.0625, so the two tests share only about 6 percent of their variance. Two tests that claim to measure the same ability should overlap heavily; as a rule of thumb, a new test should correlate around 0.7 or more with an established test of the same construct. A test that agrees so weakly with an accepted measure cannot be trusted to measure abstract reasoning. Example: Riya's new reasoning test and an established reasoning test rank her students quite differently, even though the correlation is \"significant\", so she must revise her items. Option a is right because the criterion was measured at the same time and the correlation is far too small to support the new test. Exam trap: statistical significance is not the same as practical size; a significant r of 0.25 is still weak.", "traps": {"b": "The correlation is significant, but r = 0.25 means only about 6 percent shared variance, far too little for two tests of the same ability, so concurrent validity is not satisfactory.", "c": "Predictive validity needs a criterion measured in the future; here both tests were given at the same time, so the study says nothing about predictive validity.", "d": "This is wrong on both counts: the design is concurrent, not predictive, and a correlation this low would not count as satisfactory anyway."}}, {"q": "From the passage (Classical Test Theory: every obtained score is the true score plus error of measurement; split-half, Kuder-Richardson, Cronbach alpha, Spearman-Brown, test-retest and interscorer reliability; content, concurrent, predictive and construct validity; factor analysis). Factor analysis is best suited for studying", "concept": "The answer is d: factor analysis is best suited for studying construct validity. Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) in \"Construct validity in psychological tests\", asks whether a test really measures the theoretical trait (construct) it claims to measure, such as anxiety or abstract reasoning. Because constructs cannot be observed directly, the evidence comes from many sources: correlations with related and unrelated measures, group differences, changes after experiments, and the internal structure of the test. Factor analysis, pioneered by Charles Spearman (1904) and extended by Louis Thurstone (1947, Multiple-Factor Analysis), takes a table of correlations among items or tests and finds a smaller number of underlying factors that account for them. If the items of a test meant to measure one construct load strongly on one factor, and that factor links to other tests of the same construct but not to tests of unrelated ones, construct validity is supported. The correlation of a test with a factor is called its factorial validity, which Anne Anastasi's textbook Psychological Testing treats as evidence of construct validity. Example: Arjun builds a 30-item self-esteem scale; factor analysis shows all items loading on one factor, and that factor correlates with an established self-esteem measure, supporting the claim that his scale measures self-esteem. Option d is right because factor analysis examines the internal structure and underlying dimensions of a test, which is the heart of construct validation. Exam trap: concurrent and predictive validity also use correlations, but each correlates the test with a single outside criterion; factor analysis looks at the pattern of many correlations to reveal the construct beneath them.", "traps": {"a": "Concurrent validity correlates a test with a criterion measured at the same time, such as an established test; it needs a simple validity coefficient, not factor analysis.", "b": "Predictive validity correlates test scores with a criterion measured later, such as job performance; it rests on a single validity coefficient, not on factor structure.", "c": "Content validity is judged by experts who check that the items cover the content domain properly; it is a matter of logical review, not of factor analysis."}}]});
